WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.484 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:00.484 --> 00:00:00.984 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:00.984 --> 00:00:02.420 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:13.390 align:middle line:90%


00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:14.870 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: OK, so let's start.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:17.530 align:middle line:90%
So first of all, wow.

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.010 align:middle line:90%
Thank you all for coming back.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.380 align:middle line:84%
I'm happy I didn't
push you away.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:27.440 align:middle line:84%
So before we start with today's
lectures, just a few logistics.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:31.550 align:middle line:84%
So for some reasons, you guys
are taking a holiday next week,

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:34.090 align:middle line:84%
unfortunately, so
we won't have class.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:37.640 align:middle line:84%
The week after, on the
29th, unfortunately,

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:41.080 align:middle line:84%
I'm away that week at Germany,
but we're going to have

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:46.750 align:middle line:84%
our amazing Rachel in the
back give a fantastic lecture.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:48.310 align:middle line:90%
So you'll enjoy that.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:55.358 align:middle line:84%
And then the week after,
which I believe is October 6,

00:00:55.358 --> 00:00:57.900 align:middle line:84%
I would have loved to give you
a great lecture, and I'm here.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.483 align:middle line:84%
However, you're going
to get something

00:00:59.483 --> 00:01:04.550 align:middle line:84%
even better because there is
a really, really amazing work,

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:08.690 align:middle line:84%
a new work by Oded
Regev, who does

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:13.700 align:middle line:84%
a new construct, a new factoring
algorithm, quantum factoring

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:19.100 align:middle line:84%
algorithm, so kind of improves
Shor's algorithm in some way.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.470 align:middle line:84%
It's a really, really
exciting piece of work.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:23.720 align:middle line:84%
Oded is in Paris,
but that's the one

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:27.510 align:middle line:84%
day he was able to come
here and present his work,

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.730 align:middle line:90%
and it's exactly on this class.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.060 align:middle line:84%
So I think what we should
do, we should all--

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.670 align:middle line:84%
this class will
move on October 6,

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.250 align:middle line:84%
and we'll all go to
Oded Regev's talk.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.708 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be this class,
it's going to be a colloquium,

00:01:39.708 --> 00:01:41.542 align:middle line:84%
and it's going to be a
theory reading group.

00:01:41.542 --> 00:01:42.870 align:middle line:90%
It's going to be all in one.

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:47.270 align:middle line:84%
It's going to have a very nice
format where the first hour

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:50.330 align:middle line:84%
or so is going to be kind of
a very general theory audience

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:54.750 align:middle line:84%
talk, and then we're going
to have the next two or less

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.970 align:middle line:84%
hours, depending on how
much he'll have the energy

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.800 align:middle line:84%
to speak to us,
we're going to talk

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.400 align:middle line:84%
about more details about
his algorithm and his work.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.050 align:middle line:90%
So that's October 6.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:05.503 align:middle line:90%
And then I'm going to be back.

00:02:05.503 --> 00:02:07.170 align:middle line:84%
So you're going to
have a bit of a break

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.740 align:middle line:84%
from me now, which I'm kind of
sad about, but you may be happy.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:15.210 align:middle line:84%
So let's first kind
of recap where we

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.970 align:middle line:90%
were and continue from there.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:22.380 align:middle line:84%
So last time, we
defined this new class

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.860 align:middle line:84%
of proofs, kind of going away
from the classical proofs

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.590 align:middle line:84%
to the regime of
interactive proofs.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:32.080 align:middle line:84%
We showed the power of
these interactive proofs

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.590 align:middle line:84%
by showing one protocol in
particular, the Sumcheck

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:36.610 align:middle line:90%
protocol.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.980 align:middle line:84%
I wrote the Sumcheck
protocol kind of very loosely

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:43.410 align:middle line:84%
in that board because we're
going to refer to it today

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:44.080 align:middle line:90%
again.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.900 align:middle line:84%
So the Sumcheck
protocol, just to recap,

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:50.160 align:middle line:84%
it's a way to prove that
the sum of a multivariate

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.670 align:middle line:84%
polynomial over, let's
say, a small set h--

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.770 align:middle line:84%
you can think of h
as 0,1 as an example,

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.390 align:middle line:84%
over a Boolean cube
or just a bigger cube,

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:02.320 align:middle line:84%
the sum is some value
over some finite field.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:05.470 align:middle line:84%
And of course, to compute the
sum takes a very long time.

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:06.720 align:middle line:90%
It's, like, h to the m.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.440 align:middle line:84%
You need to just
sum everything up.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.440 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out
that you can verify

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.660 align:middle line:84%
it using an interactive
proof very, very efficiently.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.790 align:middle line:84%
Assuming you have oracle
access to this function,

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:18.000 align:middle line:84%
you can compute it efficiently
on any random element

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.810 align:middle line:90%
in the field.

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:23.200 align:middle line:84%
So this is what we covered last
time, the Sumcheck protocol.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:24.930 align:middle line:84%
As I told you, this
object protocol

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:29.050 align:middle line:84%
is really the bread and
butter of proof systems.

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.030 align:middle line:84%
Almost every proof system
uses this protocol.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:33.180 align:middle line:84%
It's a beautiful
protocol, in my opinion,

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.970 align:middle line:84%
and we'll use it
throughout this course.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:40.720 align:middle line:84%
So if anyone has any questions
about the Sumcheck protocol,

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.720 align:middle line:90%
please ask.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.190 align:middle line:84%
And if it doesn't
sit with you yet,

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:49.360 align:middle line:84%
I really encourage you
to review the notes and--

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:56.140 align:middle line:84%
in the website, there's also
notes to Justin Thaler's survey

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:58.030 align:middle line:84%
which contains a
very nice exposition.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:00.050 align:middle line:84%
And also we have
now Scribe for that,

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:05.720 align:middle line:84%
so you have various resources
to look at the protocol.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:09.330 align:middle line:84%
And then we start to look at
applications for the protocol.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:13.870 align:middle line:84%
The first application we got
is to show interactive proof

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:17.260 align:middle line:84%
for Sharp-SAT, and we
showed this interactive.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:21.040 align:middle line:84%
The idea was to it was via this
technique of arithmetization

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.140 align:middle line:84%
converting kind of
a Boolean circuit

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:25.520 align:middle line:90%
into an arithmetic circuit.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.090 align:middle line:84%
We'll use this
technique today as well.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:30.970 align:middle line:84%
And then we departed and
talked about this idea

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:33.472 align:middle line:84%
of a doubly efficient
interactive proof, where

00:04:33.472 --> 00:04:35.180 align:middle line:84%
in a doubly efficient
interactive proofs,

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:38.650 align:middle line:84%
we really care about the runtime
of the prover in addition

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.100 align:middle line:90%
to the runtime of the verifier.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:43.230 align:middle line:84%
So in the classical
interactive proof setting,

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:45.570 align:middle line:84%
all the focus was on
efficient verification.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:49.145 align:middle line:84%
Nobody cared about the
runtime of the prover.

00:04:49.145 --> 00:04:51.020 align:middle line:84%
For all practical
purposes, we thought of him

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.060 align:middle line:90%
as an all powerful.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:56.065 align:middle line:84%
But doubly efficient,
interactive proof, now, yeah,

00:04:56.065 --> 00:04:57.690 align:middle line:84%
the proof, of course,
is more powerful.

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:59.857 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise the verifier would
do the work on his own.

00:04:59.857 --> 00:05:04.170 align:middle line:84%
But we don't have all-powerful
things in this world,

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.070 align:middle line:84%
unfortunately, so we also
care about the efficiency

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:07.920 align:middle line:90%
of the prover.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.820 align:middle line:84%
So he's just more
efficient, but we don't want

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:15.560 align:middle line:90%
his work to be crazy expensive.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:18.420 align:middle line:84%
So that was the doubly
efficient interactive proofs.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:21.770 align:middle line:84%
And we just started,
and we'll continue today

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:25.190 align:middle line:84%
to show how we get a doubly
efficient interactive proofs

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:31.130 align:middle line:84%
for counting triangles, for
counting triangles in a graph,

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:34.850 align:middle line:84%
and it was via technique called
the low-degree extension.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:38.180 align:middle line:84%
So now the plan for
this class today

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:41.300 align:middle line:84%
is to look at the
low-degree extension.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.680 align:middle line:84%
Again, this low-degree
extension, it's a theorem.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:44.780 align:middle line:90%
It's written here.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:46.430 align:middle line:90%
I'll explain it.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:48.890 align:middle line:84%
It's, again, the bread
and butter that actually--

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:51.230 align:middle line:84%
this low-degree extension
theorem in some sense

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:56.120 align:middle line:84%
is what makes the subject
protocol so popular, so

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:58.850 align:middle line:90%
kind of important.

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:01.430 align:middle line:84%
This theorem is what gives
the Sumcheck protocol

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:03.120 align:middle line:90%
its power in a sense.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:07.340 align:middle line:84%
So really together, the
low-degree extension theorem

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:11.840 align:middle line:84%
together with the Sumcheck is
like everything follows from it,

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:13.760 align:middle line:90%
via corollaries almost.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:20.030 align:middle line:84%
So that's what we'll see this
lecture and the next lecture.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:23.540 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what is the
low-degree extension theorem?

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:31.070 align:middle line:84%
What it says is that take any
function from H to the m to 0,1.

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:31.860 align:middle line:90%
H is just any set.

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:34.680 align:middle line:84%
It can be 0,1 to the m,
can be Boolean hypercube,

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.072 align:middle line:84%
but it can be a
bigger hypercube.

00:06:37.072 --> 00:06:39.530 align:middle line:84%
If you're more comfortable, if
it's easier for you to think

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:42.170 align:middle line:84%
of H as 0,1, just
think of it as 0,1.

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:46.097 align:middle line:84%
So take any function
from H to the m to 0,1.

00:06:46.097 --> 00:06:48.180 align:middle line:84%
So when I say any function,
it's not a polynomial,

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:49.347 align:middle line:90%
it's just a set of elements.

00:06:49.347 --> 00:06:51.570 align:middle line:84%
So when I say any function
from H of them to 0,1,

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:56.600 align:middle line:84%
you should think of it as just
an arbitrary sequence of H

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:00.060 align:middle line:84%
to the m bits, and
then just ordered them,

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:03.070 align:middle line:90%
like in a hypercube.

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:05.480 align:middle line:84%
So I have this sequence
of H to the m bits

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:07.310 align:middle line:90%
defined by this function.

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:12.380 align:middle line:84%
And the theorem says that if
you take any finite field that

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:17.410 align:middle line:84%
contains this set H, so if
H, let's say, is Boolean 0,1,

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:18.660 align:middle line:90%
you can take any finite field.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:21.840 align:middle line:84%
Every finite field contains
0,1, but more generally,

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:27.390 align:middle line:84%
any finite field, there
exists a unique polynomial.

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:30.050 align:middle line:84%
So now we move from an
arbitrary set of bits

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:35.130 align:middle line:90%
to a polynomial f from f tilde.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:38.180 align:middle line:84%
This is the extension, so we
call it from f to f tilde.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:41.730 align:middle line:84%
From f to the m, not H
to the m, but f to the m,

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:47.080 align:middle line:84%
so I extended it to f to the
m, to f such that if you look

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:52.240 align:middle line:84%
at the extended polynomial and
the small hypercube H to the m,

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:55.140 align:middle line:90%
it really is exactly f.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:59.770 align:middle line:84%
And this f tilde
has small degree,

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:06.090 align:middle line:84%
so it's degree in each variable
is the size of H minus 1.

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:09.530 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to visualize it,
here's what you should think.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:14.380 align:middle line:84%
I have a bunch of bits
sitting on some hypercube.

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:18.690 align:middle line:84%
It can be Boolean or it
can be more elements here.

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:22.050 align:middle line:84%
But I have arbitrary
sequence of bits sitting here

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:23.610 align:middle line:90%
in this hypercube.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:27.435 align:middle line:84%
And the theorem says
you can extend this

00:08:27.435 --> 00:08:34.280 align:middle line:84%
to a function over a big field,
over f to the m in a unique way.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:37.030 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if I specified,
it's a unique way in a way

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:37.929 align:middle line:90%
that it extends.

00:08:37.929 --> 00:08:41.260 align:middle line:84%
So the values are
exactly as before.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:45.820 align:middle line:84%
And the degree of this
polynomial in each variable

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:47.625 align:middle line:90%
is exactly H minus 1.

00:08:47.625 --> 00:08:50.810 align:middle line:90%


00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:52.220 align:middle line:90%
This is the theorem.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:56.720 align:middle line:84%
By the way, in the
case where H is 0,1,

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:02.130 align:middle line:84%
then the degree in each
variable is 1 because H is 0,1,

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:03.990 align:middle line:84%
so the size of it
is two degree 1.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:06.290 align:middle line:84%
This is often called
multilinear extension

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:09.560 align:middle line:84%
because the resulting
f tilde is of degree

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:14.640 align:middle line:84%
1 in each variable, in which
case it's called multilinear.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:18.340 align:middle line:84%
Any questions about
what this theorem says,

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:21.090 align:middle line:90%
the meaning of this theorem.

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:23.430 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to prove
this theorem next.

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:25.710 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to
go back to this counting

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:28.420 align:middle line:84%
triangles, which was just
kind of a motivating example.

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:31.140 align:middle line:84%
But the focus is
really this theorem.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:34.520 align:middle line:84%
And before I go
into the proof, I

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:38.820 align:middle line:84%
want to say why this thing is so
powerful is that it allows you

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.970 align:middle line:84%
to go from an arbitrary
sequence of bits

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:45.540 align:middle line:84%
to a polynomial, which
is of low degree.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:48.820 align:middle line:84%
And a polynomial of low
degree has a lot of structure,

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:51.280 align:middle line:84%
and that kind of
really helps us.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:53.850 align:middle line:84%
For example, once you have
polynomials of low degree

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:57.660 align:middle line:84%
with structures of low degree,
we can apply Sumcheck protocols.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:01.247 align:middle line:84%
So that's kind of the way we
often use Sumcheck protocol.

00:10:01.247 --> 00:10:02.580 align:middle line:90%
We're first going to go to look.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:04.015 align:middle line:90%
We have an arbitrary sequence.

00:10:04.015 --> 00:10:05.890 align:middle line:84%
We're going to take the
low-degree extension,

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:12.540 align:middle line:84%
and now we can argue we can
then use the Sumcheck protocol.

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:16.620 align:middle line:90%
Any questions before we--

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:18.126 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:10:18.126 --> 00:10:23.010 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Is another way to get
these [INAUDIBLE] maybe not

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:24.300 align:middle line:90%
for arbitrary constants.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.260 align:middle line:90%
So you take the arithmetized--

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:28.440 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Arithmetize?

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:28.940 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: f.

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:30.800 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah, right.

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:33.755 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: That should give you
a low-ish degree, lower degree,

00:10:33.755 --> 00:10:40.670 align:middle line:84%
if the depth of f is small
on the Boolean hypercube

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:41.280 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:43.030 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: So what
you're saying is good.

00:10:43.030 --> 00:10:45.380 align:middle line:84%
Let me just repeat
what you're saying.

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:49.010 align:middle line:84%
What you're saying
is if f has already--

00:10:49.010 --> 00:10:51.470 align:middle line:84%
is not arbitrary,
but actually is

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:55.085 align:middle line:84%
kind of a circuit, an
arithmetic circuit with and,

00:10:55.085 --> 00:10:57.830 align:middle line:84%
and or, with addition
and multiplication,

00:10:57.830 --> 00:11:00.420 align:middle line:84%
and it's very low
depth, like log depth,

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:06.070 align:middle line:84%
let's say, or let me call it
depth D, then just kind of think

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:07.640 align:middle line:90%
of it as over a big field.

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:10.610 align:middle line:84%
Especially if it's 0,1, you
can think of over a big field.

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:15.370 align:middle line:84%
And then the degree of this
thing will be 2 to the depth.

00:11:15.370 --> 00:11:16.750 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:19.660 align:middle line:90%
But 2 to the depth, eh.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:20.750 align:middle line:90%
So you're right.

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:23.800 align:middle line:84%
And so actually for Sharp-SAT
set, that's what we did.

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:24.980 align:middle line:90%
How did we use the Sumcheck?

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:26.120 align:middle line:90%
That's exactly what we did.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:27.953 align:middle line:84%
But then you need to
assume a lot of things.

00:11:27.953 --> 00:11:29.860 align:middle line:90%
You need to assume that--

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:30.650 align:middle line:90%
so you're right.

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:32.900 align:middle line:84%
If you start with an
f that has structure,

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:35.570 align:middle line:84%
that it's very, very low depth,
or the degree is very small,

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:36.278 align:middle line:90%
then you're done.

00:11:36.278 --> 00:11:37.300 align:middle line:90%
You already have it.

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:40.010 align:middle line:84%
But what this is saying
for any sequence,

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:43.790 align:middle line:84%
so any sequence of
bits can be arbitrary.

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:46.810 align:middle line:90%
You can put it in this way.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:50.140 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So I was just dropping
to the uniqueness claim.

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:54.578 align:middle line:84%
So for these kind of
low-depth f's, you might have

00:11:54.578 --> 00:11:55.120 align:middle line:90%
a different--

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:55.990 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Different.

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:57.490 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: It's
probably-- but I guess

00:11:57.490 --> 00:12:00.220 align:middle line:84%
the point is that degree
will prevent you, right?

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:01.210 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Exactly.

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:04.600 align:middle line:84%
So what you're saying
for the functions that

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:07.380 align:middle line:84%
are kind of natively
small degree,

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:09.900 align:middle line:84%
you can immediately
get an extension,

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:13.020 align:middle line:84%
but the degree will not be
the minimal, minimal degree.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:16.890 align:middle line:84%
So to get the minimal
degree, that's how you do it.

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:18.270 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good.

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:19.830 align:middle line:90%
Thanks.

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.930 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:24.100 align:middle line:84%
OK, I just want to remind
you, questions are great.

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:25.808 align:middle line:84%
I hope you just
witnessed it because it's

00:12:25.808 --> 00:12:27.220 align:middle line:90%
good for all of you.

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:30.150 align:middle line:90%
So please ask questions.

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:31.660 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:33.310 align:middle line:90%
Should we dive in?

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:33.830 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:38.980 align:middle line:84%
So the first thing to
notice is, actually, you

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:42.340 align:middle line:84%
should be familiar with
this theorem for the case

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:44.040 align:middle line:90%
where m equals 1.

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:48.130 align:middle line:84%
So first, just notice,
for m equals 1,

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:54.130 align:middle line:84%
this theorem is simply known
as the Lagrange interpolation

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:55.450 align:middle line:90%
theorem.

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:56.805 align:middle line:90%
This is really just Lagrange.

00:12:56.805 --> 00:13:05.890 align:middle line:90%


00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:08.180 align:middle line:84%
What does Lagrange
interpolation theorem says?

00:13:08.180 --> 00:13:12.800 align:middle line:84%
Just to recap, it says that
if you have arbitrary--

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:15.080 align:middle line:84%
if you have an f
exactly the same,

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:19.870 align:middle line:84%
I'll just write
it from H to 0,1,

00:13:19.870 --> 00:13:24.190 align:middle line:84%
then there is a unique way
of extending it to f to be

00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:26.470 align:middle line:90%
of degree H minus 1.

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:39.660 align:middle line:84%
So there is a unique f tilde
from f to f of degree H minus 1.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:42.310 align:middle line:84%
And Moreover, I'll
tell you what it is.

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:44.380 align:middle line:84%
Let me just tell you
what this f tilde is.

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:54.725 align:middle line:84%
The F tilde is simply sum
over all the h's in h--

00:13:54.725 --> 00:13:57.450 align:middle line:90%


00:13:57.450 --> 00:14:00.960 align:middle line:90%
sorry, f of h--

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:07.170 align:middle line:84%
times some polynomial
chi h of x,

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:13.470 align:middle line:84%
where this polynomial is
0 on all the h elements,

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:16.770 align:middle line:90%
except for on this specific one.

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:20.770 align:middle line:90%
So this polynomial where--

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:30.650 align:middle line:84%
so this is the f where
chi h of x is what?

00:14:30.650 --> 00:14:43.890 align:middle line:84%
Is 1, where if for
every h prime in H,

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:50.030 align:middle line:84%
it's 1 if h prime is equal
to h, and 0 otherwise.

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:50.780 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:14:50.780 --> 00:14:52.905 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Shouldn't that be
the inverse [INAUDIBLE]?

00:14:52.905 --> 00:14:55.540 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: Shouldn't
it be the inverse?

00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:59.135 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:59.135 --> 00:15:02.220 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: I want the output
to be-- so OK, let's see.

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:05.020 align:middle line:84%
What I want that if
I'm somewhere in H,

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:07.780 align:middle line:84%
so I want to say that if
I'm somewhere in H prime,

00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:14.030 align:middle line:84%
I want to get f of H.
I want f tilde of--

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:18.350 align:middle line:84%
so for any H star, I
want f tilde of H star

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:22.478 align:middle line:90%
to be f of H star.

00:15:22.478 --> 00:15:24.620 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Either 0 or 1, right?

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:27.270 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: Which
is either 0 or 1.

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:28.240 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:29.150 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:29.820 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So that's
what I'm saying.

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:30.600 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Oh, sorry.

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:32.683 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Instead of this,
it should be the inverse

00:15:32.683 --> 00:15:40.192 align:middle line:84%
so that when you apply on
the H star, it cancels.

00:15:40.192 --> 00:15:42.650 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: What do you mean,
f of H should be the inverse?

00:15:42.650 --> 00:15:45.025 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure-- you're saying
here, I should have inverse?

00:15:45.025 --> 00:15:45.670 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Yeah.

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:47.230 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Why?

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:52.125 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So the next H will give
you something that's non-zero.

00:15:52.125 --> 00:15:53.750 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: OK, so
let me try to argue.

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:55.490 align:middle line:84%
Maybe let's try to
do this together.

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:58.480 align:middle line:90%
So if this is chi--

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:02.980 align:middle line:84%
So I first define
chi of H that I'm

00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:04.580 align:middle line:90%
going to define it in a second.

00:16:04.580 --> 00:16:06.163 align:middle line:84%
Actually, I didn't
finish defining it,

00:16:06.163 --> 00:16:12.680 align:middle line:84%
but what's important to me
that on every h prime in H,

00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:16.010 align:middle line:84%
it's 1 if h prime is equal
to h and 0 otherwise.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:17.340 align:middle line:90%
Suppose I defined it.

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:18.810 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see what I get here.

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:23.720 align:middle line:84%
I have f tilde of
H star is sum fH.

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:26.860 align:middle line:90%


00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:31.600 align:middle line:84%
Chi h of H star, but
this is always 0.

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:37.150 align:middle line:84%
Except it's 1 when
h equals H star.

00:16:37.150 --> 00:16:42.130 align:middle line:84%
So it's always 0, except
for when h equals H star

00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:45.880 align:middle line:84%
and when h-- oh, you're
saying when h equals H star,

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:53.340 align:middle line:90%
then I have f of H star.

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:57.160 align:middle line:90%


00:16:57.160 --> 00:16:59.950 align:middle line:90%
So this does extend.

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:01.360 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:02.830 align:middle line:90%
So we're good?

00:17:02.830 --> 00:17:04.160 align:middle line:90%
OK, great.

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:06.760 align:middle line:84%
OK, but let me just
mention what is--

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:08.060 align:middle line:90%
I said, this is the chi.

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:09.839 align:middle line:90%
But let me actually be explicit.

00:17:09.839 --> 00:17:12.190 align:middle line:90%
So let me erase this.

00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:17.380 align:middle line:84%
The chi in the Lagrange
interpolation theorem,

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:18.859 align:middle line:90%
it satisfies this.

00:17:18.859 --> 00:17:21.700 align:middle line:84%
But let me actually tell
you exactly what it is.

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:22.730 align:middle line:90%
It's pretty simple.

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:30.670 align:middle line:84%
It's just sum over h
prime in H, and then it's,

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:37.270 align:middle line:84%
I think, H prime
minus x divided by h.

00:17:37.270 --> 00:17:39.810 align:middle line:90%
Oh, sorry, multiplication.

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:44.120 align:middle line:90%


00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:52.110 align:middle line:84%
h prime in H minus except for
h, and its h prime minus h.

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:56.670 align:middle line:84%
So first, note, this
is of degree h minus 1.

00:17:56.670 --> 00:17:59.825 align:middle line:84%
And when you look at
it for any element--

00:17:59.825 --> 00:18:02.515 align:middle line:90%


00:18:02.515 --> 00:18:12.650 align:middle line:84%
oh, yeah, for any element
h prime, it's going to be--

00:18:12.650 --> 00:18:17.870 align:middle line:84%
this is going to cancel out
unless you happen to be h.

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:19.180 align:middle line:90%
Wait, did I do it wrong?

00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:22.530 align:middle line:90%


00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:24.430 align:middle line:84%
Unless you're h,
and then you're 1.

00:18:24.430 --> 00:18:25.850 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:29.600 align:middle line:84%
OK, so this is just a recap
for Lagrange interpolation.

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:32.300 align:middle line:90%
Now, let me tell you here.

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:35.100 align:middle line:84%
So this theorem
says the same thing,

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:38.570 align:middle line:90%
but for m bits, for m variables.

00:18:38.570 --> 00:18:42.110 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out, you
know how this was f tilde?

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:47.600 align:middle line:90%
Here f tilde, of Z--

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:50.930 align:middle line:90%
Z now is in f to the m--

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:52.230 align:middle line:90%
is very similar.

00:18:52.230 --> 00:19:00.850 align:middle line:84%
It's also sum f of h1 up
to hm, so for every point

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:11.270 align:middle line:90%
times this chi h1hm of z.

00:19:11.270 --> 00:19:12.960 align:middle line:90%
And what is this?

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:19.530 align:middle line:84%
This is 1 if z-- and the
small hypercube, this

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:22.980 align:middle line:84%
is 1 if the Z is h1
up to hm and is 0.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:24.990 align:middle line:90%
Otherwise, and it extends.

00:19:24.990 --> 00:19:27.490 align:middle line:84%
So but actually, let
me be more specific.

00:19:27.490 --> 00:19:34.660 align:middle line:84%
It's really where
chi h1 up to hm

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:44.140 align:middle line:84%
of-- let me call it x1 up to
xm is just the multiplication

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:48.580 align:middle line:90%
of chi hi xi.

00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:51.550 align:middle line:90%
i goes from 1 to m.

00:19:51.550 --> 00:19:53.385 align:middle line:90%
OK, so let me move this board.

00:19:53.385 --> 00:19:57.890 align:middle line:90%


00:19:57.890 --> 00:19:59.610 align:middle line:90%
Don't let me erase this board.

00:19:59.610 --> 00:20:00.110 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:20:00.110 --> 00:20:00.950 align:middle line:90%
We'll need it.

00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:04.980 align:middle line:84%
So again, what is the
low-degree extension f tilde?

00:20:04.980 --> 00:20:09.490 align:middle line:84%
It's similar to the
univariate case.

00:20:09.490 --> 00:20:12.350 align:middle line:84%
You sum over the
entire hypercube,

00:20:12.350 --> 00:20:18.560 align:middle line:84%
and you multiply by the degree
h minus 1 in each variable.

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:22.790 align:middle line:84%
It's just the multiplication,
all of these chi i's that

00:20:22.790 --> 00:20:23.560 align:middle line:90%
were defined here.

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:27.010 align:middle line:90%


00:20:27.010 --> 00:20:27.510 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:20:27.510 --> 00:20:28.610 align:middle line:90%
So let's see if--

00:20:28.610 --> 00:20:32.770 align:middle line:84%
let me try to prove that this
works and that it's unique.

00:20:32.770 --> 00:20:38.750 align:middle line:90%


00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:41.730 align:middle line:84%
So first, what do
we need to argue?

00:20:41.730 --> 00:20:43.140 align:middle line:90%
So we have this f tilde.

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:45.980 align:middle line:84%
Now I need to argue
that it extends.

00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:48.080 align:middle line:90%
So the fact--

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:50.360 align:middle line:90%
OK, so proof.

00:20:50.360 --> 00:20:58.010 align:middle line:84%
So let's first see that for
every h1 star up to hm star in H

00:20:58.010 --> 00:21:06.420 align:middle line:84%
to the m, let's look
at f tilde of h, hm.

00:21:06.420 --> 00:21:07.140 align:middle line:90%
What is it?

00:21:07.140 --> 00:21:19.615 align:middle line:84%
It's just sum f of h1
up to hm times chi of--

00:21:19.615 --> 00:21:24.740 align:middle line:90%


00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:26.900 align:middle line:90%
so let's look at f tilde.

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:29.420 align:middle line:84%
f tilde, on any point
in the hypercube,

00:21:29.420 --> 00:21:32.180 align:middle line:84%
I defined it by star because
it's a specific point.

00:21:32.180 --> 00:21:34.910 align:middle line:90%
And I just substituted.

00:21:34.910 --> 00:21:36.570 align:middle line:90%
I defined this f tilde.

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:39.050 align:middle line:84%
I just substituted Z
because this is my Z point.

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:40.470 align:middle line:90%
And now what do I have?

00:21:40.470 --> 00:21:45.140 align:middle line:84%
I know that this is
equal to 1, if and only

00:21:45.140 --> 00:21:51.080 align:middle line:84%
if hi star is equal
to hi for every i,

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:53.250 align:middle line:84%
because this is how
the chi is defined.

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:57.610 align:middle line:84%
This is just the
multiplication of these kinds.

00:21:57.610 --> 00:21:59.590 align:middle line:90%
So therefore, that's it.

00:21:59.590 --> 00:22:03.990 align:middle line:84%
This is just f of h1
star up to hm star,

00:22:03.990 --> 00:22:06.330 align:middle line:84%
because the rest of
the terms cancel.

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:08.330 align:middle line:90%
Done.

00:22:08.330 --> 00:22:08.830 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:10.950 align:middle line:90%
So it extends.

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:11.915 align:middle line:90%
How about the degree?

00:22:11.915 --> 00:22:16.330 align:middle line:90%


00:22:16.330 --> 00:22:18.340 align:middle line:90%
What's the degree of f tilde?

00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:23.090 align:middle line:84%
It's just the degree
of these chis.

00:22:23.090 --> 00:22:26.850 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at
degree i of variable i.

00:22:26.850 --> 00:22:31.122 align:middle line:84%
It's the degree
i of chi of h1hm.

00:22:31.122 --> 00:22:37.870 align:middle line:90%


00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:38.860 align:middle line:90%
Which is what?

00:22:38.860 --> 00:22:44.200 align:middle line:84%
It's just the degree of
a single chi hi on xi,

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:55.820 align:middle line:84%
because this just factors to
the multiplication chi i and xi.

00:22:55.820 --> 00:22:59.710 align:middle line:84%
So that's just the
degree of this, which is,

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:03.100 align:middle line:84%
by what we saw there,
simply h minus 1.

00:23:03.100 --> 00:23:05.400 align:middle line:90%
Done.

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:06.570 align:middle line:90%
So it is indeed.

00:23:06.570 --> 00:23:08.430 align:middle line:90%
It extends f.

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:10.677 align:middle line:84%
The degree is correct, h
minus 1 in each variable.

00:23:10.677 --> 00:23:13.010 align:middle line:84%
The only thing that I need
to argue is that it's unique.

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:16.500 align:middle line:90%


00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:17.430 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:22.060 align:middle line:90%


00:23:22.060 --> 00:23:23.040 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:26.250 align:middle line:84%
So the uniqueness will
follow from the uniqueness

00:23:26.250 --> 00:23:28.060 align:middle line:90%
in the univariate case.

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:31.920 align:middle line:84%
So we already know from
Lagrange interpolation theorem

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:37.040 align:middle line:84%
that in the univariate
case, f tilde is unique.

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:39.080 align:middle line:84%
Now we're going to
rely on that to argue

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:43.550 align:middle line:84%
that in the multivariate
case, f tilde is unique.

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:46.240 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:48.390 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:50.430 align:middle line:90%
OK, so let's do it.

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:51.696 align:middle line:90%
So uniqueness.

00:23:51.696 --> 00:23:56.690 align:middle line:90%


00:23:56.690 --> 00:24:00.430 align:middle line:84%
So suppose for contradiction
that it's not unique.

00:24:00.430 --> 00:24:09.540 align:middle line:84%
So suppose for contradiction
that it's not unique.

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:12.260 align:middle line:84%
So there exists
two ways to extend

00:24:12.260 --> 00:24:16.290 align:middle line:90%
that agree on the hypercube.

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:20.190 align:middle line:84%
This means that if you
subtract these two,

00:24:20.190 --> 00:24:23.370 align:middle line:84%
you'll get a degree
h minus 1 polynomial

00:24:23.370 --> 00:24:25.980 align:middle line:84%
in each variable that
in the hypercube is 0.

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:31.630 align:middle line:84%
But it's not 0 because there
are two different polynomials.

00:24:31.630 --> 00:24:36.060 align:middle line:84%
So suppose for contradiction--
let me just erase this

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:37.190 align:middle line:90%
because it's in my way.

00:24:37.190 --> 00:24:44.400 align:middle line:84%
Suppose for contradiction that
there exists a g from f to the m

00:24:44.400 --> 00:24:57.040 align:middle line:84%
to f, non-zero, but g in
the hypercube is 0-- that's

00:24:57.040 --> 00:25:05.150 align:middle line:84%
the contradiction--
and of degree h minus 1

00:25:05.150 --> 00:25:06.530 align:middle line:90%
in each variable.

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:07.910 align:middle line:90%
Yes, that's a contradiction.

00:25:07.910 --> 00:25:09.740 align:middle line:84%
Say, suppose there
exist two polynomials

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:13.200 align:middle line:84%
that agree on the
hypercube of this degree.

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:14.740 align:middle line:90%
We're going to subtract them.

00:25:14.740 --> 00:25:17.090 align:middle line:84%
We're going to get
a single polynomial

00:25:17.090 --> 00:25:19.520 align:middle line:84%
that just 0 on the hypercube
because the two agreed.

00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:22.850 align:middle line:84%
But it's not 0 because they
were not the same polynomial.

00:25:22.850 --> 00:25:26.530 align:middle line:90%
What does it mean, non-zero?

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:29.310 align:middle line:84%
What does this mean, of degree
m minus 1 in each variable?

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:32.880 align:middle line:90%


00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:35.760 align:middle line:84%
The fact that it's
non-zero means

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:45.810 align:middle line:84%
that there exists some t1 up
to tm in f to the m such that g

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:50.810 align:middle line:90%
and t1 up to tm is not 0.

00:25:50.810 --> 00:25:51.310 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:53.800 align:middle line:84%
So we're starting to
think, suppose we have g.

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:58.040 align:middle line:84%
It's not 0, but it's
0 on the hypercube.

00:25:58.040 --> 00:25:59.540 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to argue,
no, it can't be.

00:25:59.540 --> 00:26:01.930 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to find an element in
the hypercube for which it's not

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:02.430 align:middle line:90%
0.

00:26:02.430 --> 00:26:07.140 align:middle line:90%


00:26:07.140 --> 00:26:09.090 align:middle line:90%
OK, how do I do that?

00:26:09.090 --> 00:26:12.210 align:middle line:84%
What I'm going to do, I'm
going to go into the hypercube

00:26:12.210 --> 00:26:15.147 align:middle line:84%
coordinate by coordinate,
kind of by induction.

00:26:15.147 --> 00:26:16.480 align:middle line:90%
And here's what I'm going to do.

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:24.260 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to say, OK, let's
look at g mt t2 up to tm.

00:26:24.260 --> 00:26:27.160 align:middle line:90%


00:26:27.160 --> 00:26:31.760 align:middle line:84%
This is non-zero because
on t1 it's not 0.

00:26:31.760 --> 00:26:41.690 align:middle line:84%
So this is non-zero
of degree H minus 1.

00:26:41.690 --> 00:26:47.810 align:middle line:84%
But from the Lagrange
interpolation theorem,

00:26:47.810 --> 00:26:51.410 align:middle line:84%
it says that if
it's not 0, there

00:26:51.410 --> 00:26:57.410 align:middle line:84%
must exist small h in
H, for which it's not 0,

00:26:57.410 --> 00:26:59.600 align:middle line:84%
because if it was 0 in all
h, there's a unique way

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:02.030 align:middle line:90%
to extend the 0 polynomial.

00:27:02.030 --> 00:27:05.840 align:middle line:84%
So because the uniqueness of
the Lagrange, it means that--

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:12.850 align:middle line:84%
so from Lagrange's theorem,
this is just univariate.

00:27:12.850 --> 00:27:22.600 align:middle line:84%
It means that there must exist
h1 such that g of h1t2 up to tm

00:27:22.600 --> 00:27:24.620 align:middle line:90%
is non-zero.

00:27:24.620 --> 00:27:26.840 align:middle line:90%
Again, why?

00:27:26.840 --> 00:27:28.290 align:middle line:90%
So, the t's are fixed.

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:29.990 align:middle line:90%
This is a univariate polynomial.

00:27:29.990 --> 00:27:38.310 align:middle line:84%
If it was 0 on H, then you could
extend it to the all zeros.

00:27:38.310 --> 00:27:40.890 align:middle line:84%
But there's another
way to extend,

00:27:40.890 --> 00:27:44.100 align:middle line:90%
because the g's not all zeros.

00:27:44.100 --> 00:27:45.900 align:middle line:90%
That would be a contradiction.

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:50.920 align:middle line:84%
So therefore, there must
exist H1 such that this is 0.

00:27:50.920 --> 00:27:54.040 align:middle line:84%
And now we're going to slowly
continue in this manner.

00:27:54.040 --> 00:28:01.940 align:middle line:84%
OK, now let's look
at gh1t3 up to tm.

00:28:01.940 --> 00:28:04.800 align:middle line:90%


00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:08.230 align:middle line:84%
So h1 is fixed in
the small cube.

00:28:08.230 --> 00:28:09.840 align:middle line:90%
These are fixed in f.

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:14.500 align:middle line:84%
It's non-zero because
on t2, it's not 0.

00:28:14.500 --> 00:28:16.650 align:middle line:90%
That's what we established.

00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:22.900 align:middle line:84%
So this is non-zero
of degree h minus 1,

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:27.130 align:middle line:84%
and therefore somewhere
in this small hypercube--

00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:31.390 align:middle line:84%
somewhere on h, sorry, it
should be non-zero from Lagrange

00:28:31.390 --> 00:28:32.540 align:middle line:90%
interpolation theorem.

00:28:32.540 --> 00:28:36.310 align:middle line:84%
So therefore from Lagrange
interpolation theorem,

00:28:36.310 --> 00:28:51.060 align:middle line:84%
there exists h2 such that
gh1h2 t3 up to tm is non-zero.

00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:54.240 align:middle line:84%
And you just continue
one by one until you

00:28:54.240 --> 00:29:00.110 align:middle line:84%
get that there exists gh1h2
up to hm that's non-zero,

00:29:00.110 --> 00:29:03.130 align:middle line:84%
and that's a
contradiction because we

00:29:03.130 --> 00:29:08.450 align:middle line:84%
assumed it is 0 everywhere
on the small hypercube.

00:29:08.450 --> 00:29:09.010 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:29:09.010 --> 00:29:09.510 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:29:09.510 --> 00:29:12.740 align:middle line:84%
So in the notes, I
have the reduction.

00:29:12.740 --> 00:29:15.330 align:middle line:84%
There's a proof that, kind
of by induction, very formal,

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:17.672 align:middle line:84%
but I think this is
good enough for class.

00:29:17.672 --> 00:29:19.380 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, it's going
to be a bit boring.

00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:19.760 align:middle line:90%
Questions.

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:20.370 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:29:20.370 --> 00:29:22.495 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Can you also just
use a counting argument?

00:29:22.495 --> 00:29:24.530 align:middle line:84%
Like, you can just
count how many

00:29:24.530 --> 00:29:27.420 align:middle line:84%
polynomials of degree
[INAUDIBLE] when there are?

00:29:27.420 --> 00:29:29.850 align:middle line:84%
You break it up
into all the terms,

00:29:29.850 --> 00:29:32.215 align:middle line:84%
and that's just equal to
the number of functions?

00:29:32.215 --> 00:29:33.090 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: OK, good.

00:29:33.090 --> 00:29:34.050 align:middle line:84%
So you're saying
maybe you can also

00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:35.590 align:middle line:84%
prove it using just
a counting algorithm

00:29:35.590 --> 00:29:37.290 align:middle line:84%
and say, if there is
too many, then you'll

00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:39.030 align:middle line:84%
have too many polynomials,
and that's a contradiction

00:29:39.030 --> 00:29:40.090 align:middle line:84%
because the number
of polynomials,

00:29:40.090 --> 00:29:42.935 align:middle line:84%
like the number of coefficients,
probably you can do something

00:29:42.935 --> 00:29:43.685 align:middle line:90%
like that as well.

00:29:43.685 --> 00:29:49.550 align:middle line:90%


00:29:49.550 --> 00:29:50.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:50.050 --> 00:29:50.550 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:29:50.550 --> 00:29:53.310 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Can I try to make the
case for the arithmetized--

00:29:53.310 --> 00:29:54.400 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Yes, please.

00:29:54.400 --> 00:29:56.510 align:middle line:90%
Good, good.

00:29:56.510 --> 00:29:58.740 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So the arithmetization,
we can compute it

00:29:58.740 --> 00:30:02.838 align:middle line:84%
on any element
[INAUDIBLE] easily,

00:30:02.838 --> 00:30:04.380 align:middle line:84%
I guess, without
looking at the truth

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:05.950 align:middle line:90%
table of the original function.

00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:06.720 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Good.

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:07.650 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: If I can
just repeat it.

00:30:07.650 --> 00:30:08.358 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah.

00:30:08.358 --> 00:30:10.330 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: If you have to
compute f on any point,

00:30:10.330 --> 00:30:12.150 align:middle line:84%
you need to look at
the entire [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:12.150 --> 00:30:12.690 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Good.

00:30:12.690 --> 00:30:13.240 align:middle line:90%
Very good.

00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:15.130 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what you're
saying is you're saying,

00:30:15.130 --> 00:30:20.020 align:middle line:90%
look, once you force me--

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:20.560 align:middle line:90%
look at me.

00:30:20.560 --> 00:30:22.050 align:middle line:90%
I don't assume anything about f.

00:30:22.050 --> 00:30:23.570 align:middle line:90%
You assume all degree.

00:30:23.570 --> 00:30:24.070 align:middle line:90%
Look at me.

00:30:24.070 --> 00:30:26.300 align:middle line:84%
I assume nothing,
and look what I get.

00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:28.810 align:middle line:84%
Now you're saying, well,
you assume nothing,

00:30:28.810 --> 00:30:30.610 align:middle line:84%
but you don't get
that much either,

00:30:30.610 --> 00:30:33.490 align:middle line:84%
because this f tilde
is so inefficient.

00:30:33.490 --> 00:30:35.770 align:middle line:90%
Note, to compute this f tilde--

00:30:35.770 --> 00:30:39.410 align:middle line:84%
where is it-- it
takes time h to the m.

00:30:39.410 --> 00:30:42.290 align:middle line:84%
You need to sum over
all the hypercube.

00:30:42.290 --> 00:30:45.050 align:middle line:84%
So computing f tilde
is really inefficient,

00:30:45.050 --> 00:30:47.270 align:middle line:84%
whereas your f tilde is
so efficient and nice.

00:30:47.270 --> 00:30:49.570 align:middle line:90%
100%, yes, you're right.

00:30:49.570 --> 00:30:52.240 align:middle line:90%


00:30:52.240 --> 00:30:53.980 align:middle line:90%
I'll give it to you Vinod, yeah?

00:30:53.980 --> 00:30:56.260 align:middle line:84%
No, but this will be
useful in settings

00:30:56.260 --> 00:30:58.240 align:middle line:84%
that the arithmetized
version will not be.

00:30:58.240 --> 00:31:00.160 align:middle line:90%
But that's a good point.

00:31:00.160 --> 00:31:01.150 align:middle line:90%
Thanks.

00:31:01.150 --> 00:31:04.300 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Is there an easy way to
bound the number of monomials?

00:31:04.300 --> 00:31:12.280 align:middle line:84%
Is there some unique f that
has at most so many monomials?

00:31:12.280 --> 00:31:14.457 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: OK, so the
question is, can we bound,

00:31:14.457 --> 00:31:16.540 align:middle line:84%
essentially asking, well,
can we make this f tilde

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:18.690 align:middle line:84%
a little more efficient
maybe, kind of?

00:31:18.690 --> 00:31:22.670 align:middle line:84%
So in general, we cannot
because in general--

00:31:22.670 --> 00:31:24.780 align:middle line:84%
it depends what you want
to assume about the f.

00:31:24.780 --> 00:31:27.190 align:middle line:84%
So I'm saying, I don't want
to make any assumptions

00:31:27.190 --> 00:31:27.700 align:middle line:90%
about the f.

00:31:27.700 --> 00:31:29.770 align:middle line:84%
And as you will
see, it's not just

00:31:29.770 --> 00:31:33.370 align:middle line:84%
because I want to just
be as generous as general

00:31:33.370 --> 00:31:35.150 align:middle line:84%
as possible for the
sake of generality.

00:31:35.150 --> 00:31:38.870 align:middle line:84%
Actually, in the way we use
it, it really has no structure.

00:31:38.870 --> 00:31:42.730 align:middle line:84%
The f we use is very
arbitrary, so we

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:46.150 align:middle line:84%
can't assume that it comes from
a low degree, whatever, nothing.

00:31:46.150 --> 00:31:49.970 align:middle line:84%
And in that case, if
you want to allow any f,

00:31:49.970 --> 00:31:53.780 align:middle line:84%
then you have to have all
the monomials because just

00:31:53.780 --> 00:31:59.090 align:middle line:84%
by a counting argument, if the
number of f's is, like, all

00:31:59.090 --> 00:32:00.298 align:middle line:90%
the possible h to the m bits.

00:32:00.298 --> 00:32:02.382 align:middle line:84%
So you have to have that
many monomials, otherwise

00:32:02.382 --> 00:32:04.100 align:middle line:84%
where would you store
that information?

00:32:04.100 --> 00:32:07.290 align:middle line:84%
So in general, you can't
do with less monomials.

00:32:07.290 --> 00:32:11.420 align:middle line:84%
However, in many
cases, you can do.

00:32:11.420 --> 00:32:14.100 align:middle line:84%
And actually, even, we'll use
it, the fact that in some cases,

00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:17.480 align:middle line:90%
you can do with less monomials.

00:32:17.480 --> 00:32:18.810 align:middle line:90%
So we'll see that.

00:32:18.810 --> 00:32:22.600 align:middle line:90%
But that's a great, great point.

00:32:22.600 --> 00:32:25.360 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:28.210 align:middle line:84%
So let me just tell you,
this is a very good theorem

00:32:28.210 --> 00:32:30.880 align:middle line:84%
to stop and ask questions
because it really

00:32:30.880 --> 00:32:32.300 align:middle line:90%
will get back to this so much.

00:32:32.300 --> 00:32:34.840 align:middle line:84%
And even if not in this
class, you'll see it in--

00:32:34.840 --> 00:32:36.790 align:middle line:90%
it will come at you.

00:32:36.790 --> 00:32:40.365 align:middle line:84%
It's a really, really important
and fundamental theorem.

00:32:40.365 --> 00:32:44.570 align:middle line:90%


00:32:44.570 --> 00:32:47.450 align:middle line:84%
So I guess, next,
I'm going to try

00:32:47.450 --> 00:32:50.510 align:middle line:84%
to convince you why it is such
an important and fundamental

00:32:50.510 --> 00:32:51.240 align:middle line:90%
theorem.

00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:56.930 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to start
with just looking back

00:32:56.930 --> 00:32:59.930 align:middle line:84%
at the example of doubly
efficient interactive proofs

00:32:59.930 --> 00:33:04.880 align:middle line:84%
for counting triangles, just
as a motivating example.

00:33:04.880 --> 00:33:05.750 align:middle line:90%
We'll do it quick.

00:33:05.750 --> 00:33:10.910 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll go into I think
the more interesting and general

00:33:10.910 --> 00:33:13.970 align:middle line:84%
way of using this low-degree
extension together

00:33:13.970 --> 00:33:16.550 align:middle line:84%
with the Sumcheck to get
doubly efficient proofs

00:33:16.550 --> 00:33:19.780 align:middle line:84%
for any low-depth
computation, so

00:33:19.780 --> 00:33:21.080 align:middle line:90%
kind of arbitrary computation.

00:33:21.080 --> 00:33:22.460 align:middle line:90%
So we'll see that next.

00:33:22.460 --> 00:33:24.490 align:middle line:84%
But let's first,
kind of as a warm-up,

00:33:24.490 --> 00:33:28.160 align:middle line:84%
look at the example
for counting triangles.

00:33:28.160 --> 00:33:30.430 align:middle line:84%
Why this low-degree
extension with Sumcheck,

00:33:30.430 --> 00:33:35.140 align:middle line:84%
how can we use these together
to count triangles in a graph?

00:33:35.140 --> 00:33:37.130 align:middle line:90%
So now, note, what's the goal?

00:33:37.130 --> 00:33:38.345 align:middle line:90%
We have a graph.

00:33:38.345 --> 00:33:39.970 align:middle line:84%
I want to know how
many triangles there

00:33:39.970 --> 00:33:40.880 align:middle line:90%
are in the graph.

00:33:40.880 --> 00:33:42.080 align:middle line:90%
Now, it's not that hard.

00:33:42.080 --> 00:33:43.540 align:middle line:90%
It takes time, n to the third.

00:33:43.540 --> 00:33:46.000 align:middle line:84%
I go over every three
pairs of vertices and check

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:47.000 align:middle line:90%
if there's a triangle.

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:49.660 align:middle line:84%
So I have an n to the
n cubed algorithm.

00:33:49.660 --> 00:33:52.310 align:middle line:84%
But let's say the verifier
can't run in time n cubed.

00:33:52.310 --> 00:33:55.990 align:middle line:84%
The poor guy, he gets the
graph, so size n squared.

00:33:55.990 --> 00:33:59.470 align:middle line:84%
All he can do is run time
n squared, essentially,

00:33:59.470 --> 00:34:02.660 align:middle line:84%
with some polylog overhead,
but only n squared.

00:34:02.660 --> 00:34:03.970 align:middle line:90%
So he can't count.

00:34:03.970 --> 00:34:06.820 align:middle line:90%
So now what does he do?

00:34:06.820 --> 00:34:08.199 align:middle line:90%
He needs help.

00:34:08.199 --> 00:34:11.590 align:middle line:84%
Now I'll show you how the
prover can help him in a way

00:34:11.590 --> 00:34:15.340 align:middle line:84%
that now, yes, he only needs
to run in time n squared

00:34:15.340 --> 00:34:17.650 align:middle line:90%
or tilde of n squared.

00:34:17.650 --> 00:34:21.510 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let's look
at that example.

00:34:21.510 --> 00:34:22.639 align:middle line:90%
So let me erase this.

00:34:22.639 --> 00:34:32.510 align:middle line:90%


00:34:32.510 --> 00:34:35.019 align:middle line:84%
So here is an example
where this is useful.

00:34:35.019 --> 00:34:38.620 align:middle line:90%


00:34:38.620 --> 00:34:39.645 align:middle line:90%
We're given a graph.

00:34:39.645 --> 00:34:44.639 align:middle line:90%


00:34:44.639 --> 00:34:47.639 align:middle line:84%
And the goal, we want a
protocol, a proof where

00:34:47.639 --> 00:34:49.590 align:middle line:84%
the prover will
convince to the verifier

00:34:49.590 --> 00:34:59.770 align:middle line:84%
that the number of
triangles in g is,

00:34:59.770 --> 00:35:03.400 align:middle line:90%
let's say, some number beta.

00:35:03.400 --> 00:35:06.480 align:middle line:90%
I want to prove for this fact.

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:08.700 align:middle line:84%
How will I-- so I'm
going to prove to you.

00:35:08.700 --> 00:35:11.190 align:middle line:90%
You guys are time of n squared.

00:35:11.190 --> 00:35:12.780 align:middle line:90%
You can only read the graph.

00:35:12.780 --> 00:35:17.780 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to help you, prove to
you that the number of triangles

00:35:17.780 --> 00:35:22.640 align:middle line:84%
is exactly beta while you
just run time n squared.

00:35:22.640 --> 00:35:24.930 align:middle line:90%
How do I do that?

00:35:24.930 --> 00:35:28.500 align:middle line:84%
Let's look at the
function f from--

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:32.150 align:middle line:90%
so suppose v is n.

00:35:32.150 --> 00:35:40.010 align:middle line:84%
So I look at the function
from 0,1 to log n to 0,1,

00:35:40.010 --> 00:35:43.130 align:middle line:90%
and this just tells me whether--

00:35:43.130 --> 00:35:44.850 align:middle line:90%
so it's like 0,1 to the log.

00:35:44.850 --> 00:35:49.760 align:middle line:84%
It's you can think of this
as being kind of n by n,

00:35:49.760 --> 00:35:53.870 align:middle line:84%
so it takes log n bits that
describe one vertex log

00:35:53.870 --> 00:35:56.600 align:middle line:84%
n bit to describe another
vertex, and it outputs 1 if

00:35:56.600 --> 00:35:58.620 align:middle line:90%
and only if there's an edge.

00:35:58.620 --> 00:36:00.360 align:middle line:90%
So you can think of each--

00:36:00.360 --> 00:36:10.280 align:middle line:84%
so f takes two vertices
i and j, and outputs 1

00:36:10.280 --> 00:36:16.220 align:middle line:84%
if there is an edge
and 0 otherwise.

00:36:16.220 --> 00:36:19.020 align:middle line:84%
So f is just the
adjacency matrix.

00:36:19.020 --> 00:36:21.660 align:middle line:84%
The adjacency matrix
is n squared bits.

00:36:21.660 --> 00:36:25.540 align:middle line:84%
This is n squared, so I just
put it in, define it this way.

00:36:25.540 --> 00:36:28.240 align:middle line:90%
So again, f takes two vertices.

00:36:28.240 --> 00:36:30.735 align:middle line:84%
It outputs 1 if there is an
edge between these vertices.

00:36:30.735 --> 00:36:33.558 align:middle line:90%


00:36:33.558 --> 00:36:39.410 align:middle line:90%
Now, so what do we need?

00:36:39.410 --> 00:36:41.150 align:middle line:90%
So now, what do we know?

00:36:41.150 --> 00:36:46.460 align:middle line:84%
We know that this f has
a low-degree extension.

00:36:46.460 --> 00:36:51.810 align:middle line:90%


00:36:51.810 --> 00:36:53.225 align:middle line:90%
So this is of degree.

00:36:53.225 --> 00:36:56.200 align:middle line:90%


00:36:56.200 --> 00:37:00.760 align:middle line:84%
Essentially this is multilinear
because I started with 0,1.

00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:07.900 align:middle line:84%
So f tilde is multilinear,
so degree 1 in each variable.

00:37:07.900 --> 00:37:12.770 align:middle line:84%
Now, why am I looking at
these adjacency matrix?

00:37:12.770 --> 00:37:13.880 align:middle line:90%
What do I want?

00:37:13.880 --> 00:37:15.850 align:middle line:90%
What do I want to prove?

00:37:15.850 --> 00:37:18.270 align:middle line:84%
How does this relate to
the number of triangles?

00:37:18.270 --> 00:37:20.770 align:middle line:84%
So the number of triangles--
we started discussing this last

00:37:20.770 --> 00:37:21.280 align:middle line:90%
time--

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:27.260 align:middle line:84%
is just the sum over
all kind of triangles,

00:37:27.260 --> 00:37:30.180 align:middle line:84%
i, j, k, or sorry, all
kind of three nodes,

00:37:30.180 --> 00:37:34.400 align:middle line:84%
i, j, k, and v. And
it's a triangle if what?

00:37:34.400 --> 00:37:47.480 align:middle line:84%
If f, i, j is one and f,
j, k is and f, i, k is one.

00:37:47.480 --> 00:37:52.370 align:middle line:84%
So I want to know how many
triplets is this thing one?

00:37:52.370 --> 00:37:55.110 align:middle line:84%
If an edge is missing
in one of them,

00:37:55.110 --> 00:37:58.330 align:middle line:84%
then this is going to be
0 because I multiplied.

00:37:58.330 --> 00:38:03.180 align:middle line:84%
So I want to know how many
of them do I have one?

00:38:03.180 --> 00:38:05.250 align:middle line:84%
And that's almost the
number of triangles,

00:38:05.250 --> 00:38:08.660 align:middle line:84%
except that I need to
divide by 6 because I

00:38:08.660 --> 00:38:11.060 align:middle line:84%
counted each triangle,
each three vertices, all

00:38:11.060 --> 00:38:11.940 align:middle line:90%
the permutations.

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:14.950 align:middle line:84%
So that's what the prover
wants to prove to the verifier.

00:38:14.950 --> 00:38:17.610 align:middle line:90%


00:38:17.610 --> 00:38:23.600 align:middle line:84%
So let me-- I said v, but let
me call it 0,1 to the log n,

00:38:23.600 --> 00:38:25.890 align:middle line:90%
which is the same.

00:38:25.890 --> 00:38:27.940 align:middle line:90%
Now I'm done.

00:38:27.940 --> 00:38:28.953 align:middle line:90%
Why am I done?

00:38:28.953 --> 00:38:30.370 align:middle line:84%
Because I'm saying,
you know what?

00:38:30.370 --> 00:38:31.980 align:middle line:90%
So how do I prove this?

00:38:31.980 --> 00:38:33.690 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to use the Sumcheck.

00:38:33.690 --> 00:38:36.560 align:middle line:84%
But you're saying, wait, but
this is not a polynomial.

00:38:36.560 --> 00:38:37.650 align:middle line:90%
No worries.

00:38:37.650 --> 00:38:40.370 align:middle line:90%
Here you go.

00:38:40.370 --> 00:38:41.930 align:middle line:90%
Now it's a polynomial.

00:38:41.930 --> 00:38:44.720 align:middle line:84%
Now, I didn't change
anything because it's only

00:38:44.720 --> 00:38:46.130 align:middle line:90%
over the small cube.

00:38:46.130 --> 00:38:52.230 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that I extended
doesn't change the sum

00:38:52.230 --> 00:38:54.530 align:middle line:90%
because of the extension.

00:38:54.530 --> 00:38:59.120 align:middle line:84%
So the prover needs to
prove that the sum of this

00:38:59.120 --> 00:39:02.310 align:middle line:84%
polynomial-- so before,
it was multilinear.

00:39:02.310 --> 00:39:04.800 align:middle line:84%
Now it's degree 2
because each variable,

00:39:04.800 --> 00:39:07.710 align:middle line:84%
we multiply 3 polynomials,
each one appears in two.

00:39:07.710 --> 00:39:10.510 align:middle line:90%
Fine, degree 2 in each variable.

00:39:10.510 --> 00:39:16.340 align:middle line:84%
And we need to prove
that the sum is beta.

00:39:16.340 --> 00:39:20.870 align:middle line:84%
This is exactly what
Sumcheck is for, precisely.

00:39:20.870 --> 00:39:22.455 align:middle line:90%
Note, what does the prover--

00:39:22.455 --> 00:39:23.580 align:middle line:90%
so what are we going to do?

00:39:23.580 --> 00:39:25.300 align:middle line:84%
We're going to do
Sumcheck protocol.

00:39:25.300 --> 00:39:27.670 align:middle line:84%
What is the runtime of the
verifier in the Sumcheck

00:39:27.670 --> 00:39:28.280 align:middle line:90%
protocol?

00:39:28.280 --> 00:39:29.230 align:middle line:90%
So they interact.

00:39:29.230 --> 00:39:33.220 align:middle line:84%
We do, every time you get rid of
one variable, one by one by one.

00:39:33.220 --> 00:39:38.380 align:middle line:84%
At the end, the verifier
needs to compute

00:39:38.380 --> 00:39:41.560 align:middle line:84%
the final polynomial,
which in our case

00:39:41.560 --> 00:39:46.930 align:middle line:84%
is this entire polynomial, and
a single point in the extension.

00:39:46.930 --> 00:39:50.620 align:middle line:84%
Namely, he needs to compute f
tilde on a random point in f

00:39:50.620 --> 00:39:56.570 align:middle line:84%
in, I guess, f
squared three times.

00:39:56.570 --> 00:39:58.910 align:middle line:84%
But that he can do
very, very easily.

00:39:58.910 --> 00:40:01.250 align:middle line:84%
Or what do you mean,
in time n squared.

00:40:01.250 --> 00:40:02.630 align:middle line:90%
Because what is f tilde?

00:40:02.630 --> 00:40:07.100 align:middle line:84%
f tilde, as Vinod said,
you need to enumerate over

00:40:07.100 --> 00:40:09.530 align:middle line:90%
all the small cube.

00:40:09.530 --> 00:40:10.220 align:middle line:90%
It's a lot.

00:40:10.220 --> 00:40:12.920 align:middle line:84%
But here are the small
cube is n squared.

00:40:12.920 --> 00:40:16.178 align:middle line:84%
So it really takes time n
squared, so order of n ,

00:40:16.178 --> 00:40:17.390 align:middle line:90%
I think it is, three times.

00:40:17.390 --> 00:40:20.640 align:middle line:84%
He needs to compute this,
but this is polylog.

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:22.243 align:middle line:90%
So by the way, throughout this--

00:40:22.243 --> 00:40:24.410 align:middle line:84%
I mentioned it last time,
but I'll mention it again.

00:40:24.410 --> 00:40:29.480 align:middle line:84%
Throughout this course, the
polylog factors, I'm not even--

00:40:29.480 --> 00:40:34.340 align:middle line:84%
these are all you can
put things in tildes.

00:40:34.340 --> 00:40:39.050 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to pay attention
too much to polylog factors.

00:40:39.050 --> 00:40:42.140 align:middle line:84%
So really what he worked,
the runtime of the verifier

00:40:42.140 --> 00:40:43.260 align:middle line:90%
is just n squared.

00:40:43.260 --> 00:40:45.960 align:middle line:84%
The communication
complexity is nothing.

00:40:45.960 --> 00:40:48.860 align:middle line:84%
m number of variables
is order log n.

00:40:48.860 --> 00:40:52.940 align:middle line:90%
d, the degree is constant.

00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:56.090 align:middle line:84%
Log f, well, don't take f
to be too long, too big.

00:40:56.090 --> 00:41:00.580 align:middle line:84%
Take f so the log
f is not small.

00:41:00.580 --> 00:41:04.190 align:middle line:84%
And note, even the prover
doesn't run in too long.

00:41:04.190 --> 00:41:08.300 align:middle line:84%
What's the runtime of the
prover in the Sumcheck protocol?

00:41:08.300 --> 00:41:12.720 align:middle line:84%
It's m, the number of variables,
log n, times h to the m.

00:41:12.720 --> 00:41:15.010 align:middle line:84%
Now, h to the m seems
big, but again, it's

00:41:15.010 --> 00:41:19.190 align:middle line:84%
n squared, times the time
it takes to compute f once,

00:41:19.190 --> 00:41:22.410 align:middle line:90%
which is, again, n squared.

00:41:22.410 --> 00:41:23.600 align:middle line:90%
So it is polynomial.

00:41:23.600 --> 00:41:25.815 align:middle line:84%
And now the verifier runs
in time of n squared.

00:41:25.815 --> 00:41:28.590 align:middle line:90%


00:41:28.590 --> 00:41:30.880 align:middle line:90%
So that's just to illustrate.

00:41:30.880 --> 00:41:34.130 align:middle line:90%


00:41:34.130 --> 00:41:34.680 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:41:34.680 --> 00:41:36.513 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE] runtime
has to be [INAUDIBLE] cubed

00:41:36.513 --> 00:41:37.200 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:39.090 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah,
right, and he is.

00:41:39.090 --> 00:41:42.060 align:middle line:84%
He is actually n to the
fourth in this example,

00:41:42.060 --> 00:41:46.530 align:middle line:84%
because he needs
to compute this--

00:41:46.530 --> 00:41:47.167 align:middle line:90%
OK, let's see.

00:41:47.167 --> 00:41:48.000 align:middle line:90%
He's more, actually.

00:41:48.000 --> 00:41:49.450 align:middle line:90%
He needs to compute this thing.

00:41:49.450 --> 00:41:52.743 align:middle line:84%
So he needs to do it
for every i, j, k in--

00:41:52.743 --> 00:41:56.820 align:middle line:90%


00:41:56.820 --> 00:41:58.840 align:middle line:84%
not just the compute, to
do the entire Sumcheck.

00:41:58.840 --> 00:42:01.890 align:middle line:84%
So in the Sumcheck,
he needs to h

00:42:01.890 --> 00:42:08.090 align:middle line:84%
to the m, which is m here is
going to be 3 log n in this.

00:42:08.090 --> 00:42:11.380 align:middle line:84%
So it's already m
cubed, and then he

00:42:11.380 --> 00:42:15.170 align:middle line:84%
needs to compute t of f, the
polynomial, which is n squared.

00:42:15.170 --> 00:42:17.220 align:middle line:90%
So he's n to the fifth.

00:42:17.220 --> 00:42:21.600 align:middle line:84%
Because computing this once
is n squared without the sum.

00:42:21.600 --> 00:42:26.060 align:middle line:84%
But he also needs to run over
all the sum and then also log

00:42:26.060 --> 00:42:26.560 align:middle line:90%
factors.

00:42:26.560 --> 00:42:28.710 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, he's running more.

00:42:28.710 --> 00:42:31.020 align:middle line:84%
But it's considered
doubly efficient

00:42:31.020 --> 00:42:34.450 align:middle line:84%
because he's still
polynomial overhead.

00:42:34.450 --> 00:42:34.960 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:42:34.960 --> 00:42:36.890 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Is there a way
to get into the omega

00:42:36.890 --> 00:42:39.300 align:middle line:84%
or something [INAUDIBLE]
runtime [INAUDIBLE] that's

00:42:39.300 --> 00:42:41.167 align:middle line:90%
the actual runtime?

00:42:41.167 --> 00:42:42.510 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Even n cubed.

00:42:42.510 --> 00:42:44.940 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah, even
n cubed, you're saying.

00:42:44.940 --> 00:42:46.590 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, currently.

00:42:46.590 --> 00:42:51.120 align:middle line:84%
So n cubed, you can get
it using recent kind

00:42:51.120 --> 00:42:56.310 align:middle line:84%
of really nice works, with
just optimizing a little bit.

00:42:56.310 --> 00:43:01.000 align:middle line:84%
That's the work of Dan Sung and
friends, they get a linear time.

00:43:01.000 --> 00:43:02.350 align:middle line:90%
So they can get an n cubed.

00:43:02.350 --> 00:43:04.170 align:middle line:90%
I don't know how--

00:43:04.170 --> 00:43:06.700 align:middle line:84%
but it may be that for this
specific protocol it's possible.

00:43:06.700 --> 00:43:07.658 align:middle line:90%
Actually, I don't know.

00:43:07.658 --> 00:43:10.100 align:middle line:90%
It's a good question.

00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:12.900 align:middle line:84%
But let me, actually, before
you get too excited about it--

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:15.440 align:middle line:90%
so yeah.

00:43:15.440 --> 00:43:18.260 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE] the number
of rounds is log n, right?

00:43:18.260 --> 00:43:20.170 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: The number
of rounds is log n.

00:43:20.170 --> 00:43:22.140 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]
actually get a--

00:43:22.140 --> 00:43:22.890 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Great.

00:43:22.890 --> 00:43:23.450 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:43:23.450 --> 00:43:25.170 align:middle line:90%
Question of two messages.

00:43:25.170 --> 00:43:29.150 align:middle line:84%
So when I finished--
where's Gabe?

00:43:29.150 --> 00:43:31.536 align:middle line:84%
So you came to me
last time, right?

00:43:31.536 --> 00:43:34.230 align:middle line:84%
So when I started talking
about this last lecture,

00:43:34.230 --> 00:43:37.430 align:middle line:84%
Gabe came to me, rightly
so, and said, well--

00:43:37.430 --> 00:43:40.680 align:middle line:84%
we just started talking about
it, and he's like, well, OK,

00:43:40.680 --> 00:43:42.930 align:middle line:84%
but you can do it
so much easier.

00:43:42.930 --> 00:43:44.510 align:middle line:90%
And it's true.

00:43:44.510 --> 00:43:48.990 align:middle line:84%
This was kind of as
an example to show.

00:43:48.990 --> 00:43:52.260 align:middle line:84%
But actually, going since
you asked the question also,

00:43:52.260 --> 00:43:54.500 align:middle line:84%
actually, there's very
much simpler protocol

00:43:54.500 --> 00:43:55.590 align:middle line:90%
that Gabe noticed.

00:43:55.590 --> 00:43:59.390 align:middle line:90%
So let me just write it here.

00:43:59.390 --> 00:44:04.340 align:middle line:84%
The idea is that the prover will
only send two matrices over.

00:44:04.340 --> 00:44:06.140 align:middle line:84%
So it's a bit
communication complexity.

00:44:06.140 --> 00:44:08.060 align:middle line:84%
Here, the communication
complexity is polylog.

00:44:08.060 --> 00:44:11.240 align:middle line:84%
But if we just care
about verifier runtime

00:44:11.240 --> 00:44:13.470 align:middle line:84%
and we don't care about the
communication complexity,

00:44:13.470 --> 00:44:17.210 align:middle line:84%
then the prover can just
send the verifier the--

00:44:17.210 --> 00:44:20.070 align:middle line:84%
so let A be the
adjacency matrix.

00:44:20.070 --> 00:44:23.390 align:middle line:84%
Let me now instead of f write it
in the more classical notation

00:44:23.390 --> 00:44:29.130 align:middle line:84%
of a matrix A, the adjacency
matrix, where there's a 1,

00:44:29.130 --> 00:44:31.280 align:middle line:84%
if and only if there's
an edge, and 0 otherwise.

00:44:31.280 --> 00:44:39.440 align:middle line:84%
The prover will send the matrix
A 2, which is A squared--

00:44:39.440 --> 00:44:41.830 align:middle line:84%
he can also send A, but
the verifier knows A--

00:44:41.830 --> 00:44:45.040 align:middle line:90%
and A3, which is A cubed.

00:44:45.040 --> 00:44:48.570 align:middle line:90%


00:44:48.570 --> 00:44:54.560 align:middle line:84%
Now, the number of triangles
is just the trace of A cubed.

00:44:54.560 --> 00:44:56.040 align:middle line:90%
So it's very easy to compute.

00:44:56.040 --> 00:44:56.820 align:middle line:90%
You just compute.

00:44:56.820 --> 00:44:59.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, how do you know
that this is A squared

00:44:59.970 --> 00:45:02.000 align:middle line:90%
and this is A cubed?

00:45:02.000 --> 00:45:04.260 align:middle line:84%
Well, we saw last
time a protocol,

00:45:04.260 --> 00:45:06.650 align:middle line:84%
a randomized protocol
for checking equality

00:45:06.650 --> 00:45:10.850 align:middle line:84%
between matrices via
this Reed-Solomon kind

00:45:10.850 --> 00:45:12.500 align:middle line:90%
of decoding idea.

00:45:12.500 --> 00:45:13.590 align:middle line:90%
So you do that.

00:45:13.590 --> 00:45:14.940 align:middle line:90%
So there you go.

00:45:14.940 --> 00:45:18.250 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: That's really, really
nice, but I'm not happy.

00:45:18.250 --> 00:45:21.428 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: But then
blame Gabe, of course.

00:45:21.428 --> 00:45:22.880 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:22.880 --> 00:45:24.458 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Exactly.

00:45:24.458 --> 00:45:25.000 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

00:45:25.000 --> 00:45:34.150 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:34.150 --> 00:45:35.930 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: You can get--

00:45:35.930 --> 00:45:36.430 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:45:36.430 --> 00:45:41.140 align:middle line:90%


00:45:41.140 --> 00:45:45.430 align:middle line:84%
So there's a really
beautiful work of R, R, R,

00:45:45.430 --> 00:45:48.940 align:middle line:84%
and they get constant
round complexity.

00:45:48.940 --> 00:45:52.400 align:middle line:84%
And so using their particle,
probably you can get very good.

00:45:52.400 --> 00:45:54.170 align:middle line:84%
But I need to think
about the parameters.

00:45:54.170 --> 00:45:58.660 align:middle line:84%
I think you can get in
constant round probably really

00:45:58.660 --> 00:46:03.410 align:middle line:84%
good parameters, but I need
to think about the details.

00:46:03.410 --> 00:46:05.480 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:46:05.480 --> 00:46:07.880 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:46:07.880 --> 00:46:09.560 align:middle line:84%
So this was just a
motivating example

00:46:09.560 --> 00:46:12.620 align:middle line:84%
to show you that you can take
something also with no structure

00:46:12.620 --> 00:46:15.097 align:middle line:84%
whatsoever, like
adjacency matrix.

00:46:15.097 --> 00:46:15.930 align:middle line:90%
It's a no structure.

00:46:15.930 --> 00:46:16.880 align:middle line:90%
It's arbitrary.

00:46:16.880 --> 00:46:20.850 align:middle line:84%
But using the technique
of a low-degree extension,

00:46:20.850 --> 00:46:23.960 align:middle line:84%
you can add structure to it, and
then you can use the Sumcheck.

00:46:23.960 --> 00:46:27.450 align:middle line:90%


00:46:27.450 --> 00:46:31.900 align:middle line:84%
So more generally,
I want to say,

00:46:31.900 --> 00:46:34.650 align:middle line:84%
I think what's really
the power of this

00:46:34.650 --> 00:46:38.070 align:middle line:84%
is in error correcting codes,
what's nice about error

00:46:38.070 --> 00:46:41.290 align:middle line:84%
correcting codes
is if you cheat,

00:46:41.290 --> 00:46:44.010 align:middle line:84%
you need to cheat
everywhere in the sense.

00:46:44.010 --> 00:46:45.990 align:middle line:90%
Things kind of percolate.

00:46:45.990 --> 00:46:47.910 align:middle line:84%
In proof system,
this kind of thing

00:46:47.910 --> 00:46:51.270 align:middle line:84%
is very, very useful
because we want to--

00:46:51.270 --> 00:46:54.900 align:middle line:84%
proof systems about
catching cheaters.

00:46:54.900 --> 00:46:57.190 align:middle line:84%
When we construct a
constructive proof,

00:46:57.190 --> 00:47:00.700 align:middle line:84%
our goal is to catch a
prover, a cheating prover.

00:47:00.700 --> 00:47:03.345 align:middle line:90%
And so you want some encoding.

00:47:03.345 --> 00:47:05.230 align:middle line:84%
You want to say, if
you cheat somewhere,

00:47:05.230 --> 00:47:06.970 align:middle line:90%
he has to cheat everywhere.

00:47:06.970 --> 00:47:10.050 align:middle line:90%
So this idea of having--

00:47:10.050 --> 00:47:12.450 align:middle line:84%
in some sense,
you want kind of--

00:47:12.450 --> 00:47:13.740 align:middle line:90%
if you want the proof to--

00:47:13.740 --> 00:47:16.670 align:middle line:90%


00:47:16.670 --> 00:47:17.990 align:middle line:90%
it's very natural.

00:47:17.990 --> 00:47:21.360 align:middle line:84%
Some encoding comes
into proof systems,

00:47:21.360 --> 00:47:24.560 align:middle line:84%
and we'll see that next when
we look at the GKR protocol

00:47:24.560 --> 00:47:27.860 align:middle line:90%
for low-depth computation.

00:47:27.860 --> 00:47:29.790 align:middle line:90%
So that's coming next.

00:47:29.790 --> 00:47:32.270 align:middle line:84%
Now, before I start
this protocol,

00:47:32.270 --> 00:47:33.820 align:middle line:90%
I have a question for you.

00:47:33.820 --> 00:47:36.320 align:middle line:84%
So last time-- this
is a long class.

00:47:36.320 --> 00:47:37.320 align:middle line:90%
It's a three hour class.

00:47:37.320 --> 00:47:40.228 align:middle line:90%
It's tiring.

00:47:40.228 --> 00:47:42.720 align:middle line:84%
And it's technical, which
is even more tiring.

00:47:42.720 --> 00:47:45.530 align:middle line:84%
So now I want to
ask you two options.

00:47:45.530 --> 00:47:49.020 align:middle line:84%
Option 1, we'll take two
breaks, one after one hour,

00:47:49.020 --> 00:47:51.200 align:middle line:90%
one after the second hour.

00:47:51.200 --> 00:47:52.410 align:middle line:90%
It will give you--

00:47:52.410 --> 00:47:57.110 align:middle line:84%
or we take one in the middle,
which is maybe a little longer.

00:47:57.110 --> 00:48:02.820 align:middle line:84%
How many people are for two kind
of short breaks to decompose?

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:05.466 align:middle line:84%
Let's take a break now for five
minutes and then we'll do GKR.

00:48:05.466 --> 00:48:08.800 align:middle line:90%


00:48:08.800 --> 00:48:10.552 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:48:10.552 --> 00:48:18.368 align:middle line:84%
So so far, the applications, we
saw for the Sumcheck and even

00:48:18.368 --> 00:48:19.910 align:middle line:84%
if you want the
low-degree extension,

00:48:19.910 --> 00:48:21.460 align:middle line:90%
were both kind of counting.

00:48:21.460 --> 00:48:26.600 align:middle line:84%
We said, let's
count the Sharp-SAT,

00:48:26.600 --> 00:48:28.790 align:middle line:84%
let's count the
number of triangles.

00:48:28.790 --> 00:48:32.780 align:middle line:84%
So you may think, OK, well,
Sumcheck is about counting,

00:48:32.780 --> 00:48:36.513 align:middle line:84%
so yeah, I can see how you can
do counting problems by either

00:48:36.513 --> 00:48:37.930 align:middle line:84%
they're already
kind of low degree

00:48:37.930 --> 00:48:39.910 align:middle line:84%
and you just apply
Sumcheck automatically,

00:48:39.910 --> 00:48:41.620 align:middle line:84%
or they're not low
degree and then you

00:48:41.620 --> 00:48:44.860 align:middle line:84%
do a low-degree extension,
and now you have the Sumcheck.

00:48:44.860 --> 00:48:48.490 align:middle line:84%
So maybe you can do counting
things, but that's it.

00:48:48.490 --> 00:48:50.980 align:middle line:84%
So now what I want
to show you next

00:48:50.980 --> 00:48:53.810 align:middle line:84%
is that, no, actually you
can do a ton with that.

00:48:53.810 --> 00:48:57.050 align:middle line:84%
So just the low-degree
extension and Sumcheck together,

00:48:57.050 --> 00:48:57.963 align:middle line:90%
that's all you need.

00:48:57.963 --> 00:48:59.380 align:middle line:84%
And what I'm going
to show you now

00:48:59.380 --> 00:49:03.010 align:middle line:84%
is how to do a doubly
efficient interactive proof

00:49:03.010 --> 00:49:06.640 align:middle line:90%
for all low-depth circuits.

00:49:06.640 --> 00:49:08.630 align:middle line:90%
So let me explain.

00:49:08.630 --> 00:49:12.490 align:middle line:84%
So suppose you have
here some circuit

00:49:12.490 --> 00:49:17.590 align:middle line:84%
C. It's really, really
big, but it's shallow.

00:49:17.590 --> 00:49:23.140 align:middle line:84%
OK, so think of this circuit
size S, which is really big.

00:49:23.140 --> 00:49:30.590 align:middle line:84%
The depth is d,
which is very small.

00:49:30.590 --> 00:49:33.730 align:middle line:84%
Now what I'm going
to show you is

00:49:33.730 --> 00:49:38.260 align:middle line:84%
a doubly efficient, interactive
proof where the verifier runs

00:49:38.260 --> 00:49:45.742 align:middle line:84%
in time only d, or linear
in d or d and poly log in S.

00:49:45.742 --> 00:49:48.200 align:middle line:84%
And the prover, of course, he
needs to compute the circuit.

00:49:48.200 --> 00:49:51.040 align:middle line:84%
He runs in time S. He has
to run the computation.

00:49:51.040 --> 00:49:53.865 align:middle line:84%
And only poly
overhead, so poly of S.

00:49:53.865 --> 00:49:55.240 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
show you a doubly

00:49:55.240 --> 00:49:57.530 align:middle line:84%
efficient interactive proof
for any bounded depth.

00:49:57.530 --> 00:49:58.030 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:49:58.030 --> 00:49:59.290 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Just to clarify
here, circuit size

00:49:59.290 --> 00:50:00.440 align:middle line:90%
is just the number of gates?

00:50:00.440 --> 00:50:00.610 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Good.

00:50:00.610 --> 00:50:02.140 align:middle line:84%
The circuit size is just
number of gates, yeah.

00:50:02.140 --> 00:50:03.130 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Thank you.

00:50:03.130 --> 00:50:04.030 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah.

00:50:04.030 --> 00:50:09.230 align:middle line:84%
Now, note, this already
seems weird because--

00:50:09.230 --> 00:50:11.230 align:middle line:84%
OK, so the prover,
I said, the goal

00:50:11.230 --> 00:50:13.540 align:middle line:84%
is the prover is going
to convince the verifier

00:50:13.540 --> 00:50:20.970 align:middle line:84%
that, let's say, so the goal is
to convince the verifier that C

00:50:20.970 --> 00:50:23.970 align:middle line:90%
of x equals, let's say, 1.

00:50:23.970 --> 00:50:27.270 align:middle line:90%


00:50:27.270 --> 00:50:32.550 align:middle line:84%
Think of C as any Boolean
circuit of small depth.

00:50:32.550 --> 00:50:35.040 align:middle line:84%
And the verifier should
be very efficient.

00:50:35.040 --> 00:50:38.115 align:middle line:84%
But now you're saying, just
reading C is not efficient.

00:50:38.115 --> 00:50:39.490 align:middle line:84%
If you have an
arbitrary circuit,

00:50:39.490 --> 00:50:42.030 align:middle line:84%
just reading the circuit
is not efficient.

00:50:42.030 --> 00:50:42.910 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

00:50:42.910 --> 00:50:45.360 align:middle line:84%
So for this to be
meaningful, C needs

00:50:45.360 --> 00:50:47.500 align:middle line:84%
to have a succinct
representation.

00:50:47.500 --> 00:50:49.470 align:middle line:84%
So it needs to be kind
of a uniform circuit

00:50:49.470 --> 00:50:52.060 align:middle line:84%
that you can specify
it succinctly.

00:50:52.060 --> 00:50:54.570 align:middle line:84%
And indeed we will
assume that C is

00:50:54.570 --> 00:51:05.380 align:middle line:84%
what's called logspace uniform,
which essentially means

00:51:05.380 --> 00:51:09.160 align:middle line:84%
that there's a logspace
machine, a log S space

00:51:09.160 --> 00:51:15.040 align:middle line:84%
machine, a uniform
Turing machine that

00:51:15.040 --> 00:51:17.060 align:middle line:90%
generates the circuit.

00:51:17.060 --> 00:51:19.900 align:middle line:84%
But actually, for now,
don't think about.

00:51:19.900 --> 00:51:23.100 align:middle line:84%
Just suppose it's just kind of
there's some uniform condition.

00:51:23.100 --> 00:51:25.990 align:middle line:84%
So the verifier has succinct
representation of the C,

00:51:25.990 --> 00:51:28.030 align:middle line:84%
because it will only
come much, much later

00:51:28.030 --> 00:51:29.860 align:middle line:90%
when you'll see this--

00:51:29.860 --> 00:51:31.660 align:middle line:84%
you'll need to deal
with this issue.

00:51:31.660 --> 00:51:37.150 align:middle line:84%
So the goal is for the prover
to prove to the verifier

00:51:37.150 --> 00:51:39.790 align:middle line:90%
that C of x equals 1.

00:51:39.790 --> 00:51:43.360 align:middle line:84%
And the idea, the way we're
going to do this essentially is,

00:51:43.360 --> 00:51:48.400 align:middle line:84%
the high-level idea,
is we're going to--

00:51:48.400 --> 00:51:51.550 align:middle line:90%
so I want to catch the prover.

00:51:51.550 --> 00:51:52.250 align:middle line:90%
He's cheating.

00:51:52.250 --> 00:51:53.120 align:middle line:90%
How am I going to catch him?

00:51:53.120 --> 00:51:54.495 align:middle line:84%
I want to make
sure he's correct.

00:51:54.495 --> 00:51:57.710 align:middle line:84%
So the prover comes to me and
says, look, C of x equal 1.

00:51:57.710 --> 00:51:59.830 align:middle line:90%
The value here is 1.

00:51:59.830 --> 00:52:03.520 align:middle line:84%
I want to reduce the
claim on the output value

00:52:03.520 --> 00:52:07.470 align:middle line:84%
to a claim about something
in one layer below,

00:52:07.470 --> 00:52:09.300 align:middle line:90%
by the small cell protocol.

00:52:09.300 --> 00:52:13.050 align:middle line:84%
And then I want to reduce
the claim about something

00:52:13.050 --> 00:52:15.507 align:middle line:84%
about layer below to layer
below and layer below

00:52:15.507 --> 00:52:18.090 align:middle line:84%
and layer below and layer below
and so on, until I get a claim

00:52:18.090 --> 00:52:21.090 align:middle line:84%
about the input, which the
verifier can compute on its own.

00:52:21.090 --> 00:52:22.670 align:middle line:90%
That's kind of the idea.

00:52:22.670 --> 00:52:25.030 align:middle line:84%
So for this, the
first thing we assume,

00:52:25.030 --> 00:52:30.620 align:middle line:84%
and this is without loss of
generality, that C is layered.

00:52:30.620 --> 00:52:33.380 align:middle line:90%


00:52:33.380 --> 00:52:34.740 align:middle line:90%
What do I mean by layered?

00:52:34.740 --> 00:52:36.060 align:middle line:90%
There's an output gate.

00:52:36.060 --> 00:52:38.630 align:middle line:84%
There's kind of that
let's call it layer 0.

00:52:38.630 --> 00:52:41.420 align:middle line:84%
And then you go to
gate, and then there's

00:52:41.420 --> 00:52:44.130 align:middle line:84%
kind of wires to
one layer below.

00:52:44.130 --> 00:52:45.227 align:middle line:90%
We'll call it layer 1.

00:52:45.227 --> 00:52:47.060 align:middle line:84%
And then there are gates
to one layer below.

00:52:47.060 --> 00:52:48.018 align:middle line:90%
We'll call the layer 2.

00:52:48.018 --> 00:52:51.470 align:middle line:84%
So there's no kind of long
gate that goes from the input

00:52:51.470 --> 00:52:54.440 align:middle line:84%
straight to-- it's
really layer by layer.

00:52:54.440 --> 00:52:56.133 align:middle line:90%
So that's the first assumption.

00:52:56.133 --> 00:52:57.300 align:middle line:90%
So we can talk about layers.

00:52:57.300 --> 00:53:06.020 align:middle line:84%
So what it means is
that gate in layer i

00:53:06.020 --> 00:53:23.520 align:middle line:84%
is connected only to
gates in layer i plus 1,

00:53:23.520 --> 00:53:31.040 align:middle line:84%
where we think of i
equals 0 is output

00:53:31.040 --> 00:53:34.090 align:middle line:90%
and i equal d is input layer.

00:53:34.090 --> 00:53:39.713 align:middle line:90%


00:53:39.713 --> 00:53:41.505 align:middle line:84%
So this is just for
the sake of simplicity.

00:53:41.505 --> 00:53:45.180 align:middle line:84%
You can always add
dummy gates to make

00:53:45.180 --> 00:53:49.330 align:middle line:84%
sure that each gate is only
connected to one layer below.

00:53:49.330 --> 00:53:53.440 align:middle line:84%
If before it was connected to
someone closer to the input,

00:53:53.440 --> 00:53:58.570 align:middle line:84%
you just add dummy
gates to go up, up, up.

00:53:58.570 --> 00:54:03.400 align:middle line:84%
So again, let me just make
sure everyone's on board.

00:54:03.400 --> 00:54:05.900 align:middle line:84%
When I say a gate is in layer
i, essentially what I mean,

00:54:05.900 --> 00:54:09.830 align:middle line:84%
the depth from there to the
input, the number, the path,

00:54:09.830 --> 00:54:11.170 align:middle line:90%
the length of the path is i.

00:54:11.170 --> 00:54:16.150 align:middle line:84%
So any gate that is kind of
i away from the input, it's

00:54:16.150 --> 00:54:20.080 align:middle line:84%
connected to gates that are only
i minus 1 away from the input.

00:54:20.080 --> 00:54:22.490 align:middle line:84%
So that's an assumption
I'm going to make.

00:54:22.490 --> 00:54:26.650 align:middle line:84%
And the way I can realize it is
by just if the original circuit

00:54:26.650 --> 00:54:28.810 align:middle line:84%
was not in this way,
I'm just going to add

00:54:28.810 --> 00:54:30.070 align:middle line:90%
dummy gates in the middle.

00:54:30.070 --> 00:54:33.070 align:middle line:84%
So if a circuit at layer 3
was connected immediately

00:54:33.070 --> 00:54:35.810 align:middle line:84%
to the input, I'm going to
just copy the input in 1, 2,

00:54:35.810 --> 00:54:39.260 align:middle line:84%
and then I'm going
to connect it.

00:54:39.260 --> 00:54:42.490 align:middle line:84%
So things are going
to be layered.

00:54:42.490 --> 00:54:45.270 align:middle line:90%
Questions about this assumption?

00:54:45.270 --> 00:54:45.850 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:54:45.850 --> 00:54:50.830 align:middle line:84%
So now the circuit is layered,
and now what I want to do,

00:54:50.830 --> 00:54:51.640 align:middle line:90%
I want to reduce.

00:54:51.640 --> 00:54:53.860 align:middle line:84%
So now I'm going to have
an interactive protocol.

00:54:53.860 --> 00:55:07.810 align:middle line:84%
So the idea in GKR is to have
an interactive protocol that

00:55:07.810 --> 00:55:25.570 align:middle line:84%
consists of d sub protocol, and
each sub protocol essentially

00:55:25.570 --> 00:55:30.700 align:middle line:84%
reduces a claim
about some layer i

00:55:30.700 --> 00:55:34.570 align:middle line:84%
to a claim about layer i plus
1, so a claim about the layer

00:55:34.570 --> 00:55:37.800 align:middle line:90%
that's one closer to the input.

00:55:37.800 --> 00:55:42.610 align:middle line:84%
So these are how these
sub protocols will go.

00:55:42.610 --> 00:55:45.040 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to explain the
GKR protocol formally,

00:55:45.040 --> 00:55:47.280 align:middle line:90%
but this is just intuition.

00:55:47.280 --> 00:55:51.610 align:middle line:90%


00:55:51.610 --> 00:55:55.260 align:middle line:90%
So let me take this, actually.

00:55:55.260 --> 00:56:04.790 align:middle line:90%


00:56:04.790 --> 00:56:06.550 align:middle line:90%
So the first thing is--

00:56:06.550 --> 00:56:11.930 align:middle line:90%


00:56:11.930 --> 00:56:15.720 align:middle line:84%
here is here is
kind of the idea.

00:56:15.720 --> 00:56:18.210 align:middle line:84%
So, again, this is
not the protocol.

00:56:18.210 --> 00:56:23.700 align:middle line:84%
I'm giving you an intuition
of, helping you understand

00:56:23.700 --> 00:56:24.650 align:middle line:90%
where I'm going to go.

00:56:24.650 --> 00:56:28.013 align:middle line:84%
So we want to prove
a cheating prover.

00:56:28.013 --> 00:56:28.930 align:middle line:90%
Suppose he's cheating.

00:56:28.930 --> 00:56:32.130 align:middle line:84%
He's trying to argue
that C of x equals 1.

00:56:32.130 --> 00:56:33.905 align:middle line:90%
But think of it, it's not.

00:56:33.905 --> 00:56:34.780 align:middle line:90%
We want to catch him.

00:56:34.780 --> 00:56:36.040 align:middle line:90%
How do we catch him?

00:56:36.040 --> 00:56:38.310 align:middle line:84%
If he's cheating,
how do we catch him?

00:56:38.310 --> 00:56:42.240 align:middle line:84%
So one idea is to say,
well, if he's cheating here,

00:56:42.240 --> 00:56:44.670 align:middle line:84%
he must-- so let's say
this is an AND gate.

00:56:44.670 --> 00:56:47.670 align:middle line:84%
Suppose it's 0, but
he's claiming it's 1.

00:56:47.670 --> 00:56:51.600 align:middle line:84%
So it means that I'm going
to ask him, you know what?

00:56:51.600 --> 00:56:54.310 align:middle line:84%
It can't be, if I tell
him, give me both of these.

00:56:54.310 --> 00:56:55.780 align:middle line:90%
He also must be cheating.

00:56:55.780 --> 00:56:58.020 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to tell him,
you know what, 1 is 1.

00:56:58.020 --> 00:57:01.110 align:middle line:84%
So in that case, you claim
that both of these are 1.

00:57:01.110 --> 00:57:01.990 align:middle line:90%
He's like, yeah.

00:57:01.990 --> 00:57:04.455 align:middle line:84%
Or if it was an OR,
I'm saying, this is 1,

00:57:04.455 --> 00:57:06.330 align:middle line:84%
so you claim that one
of them is [INAUDIBLE].

00:57:06.330 --> 00:57:07.560 align:middle line:90%
He's like, yeah.

00:57:07.560 --> 00:57:10.890 align:middle line:90%
But in reality, they're both 0.

00:57:10.890 --> 00:57:12.670 align:middle line:84%
So now, what I can
say, you know what?

00:57:12.670 --> 00:57:13.240 align:middle line:90%
OK, fine.

00:57:13.240 --> 00:57:14.790 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to choose
one of them at random.

00:57:14.790 --> 00:57:15.290 align:middle line:90%
Really?

00:57:15.290 --> 00:57:15.900 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:57:15.900 --> 00:57:17.470 align:middle line:90%
Tell me that this is a 1.

00:57:17.470 --> 00:57:23.550 align:middle line:90%


00:57:23.550 --> 00:57:25.340 align:middle line:90%
One of these has to be a cheat.

00:57:25.340 --> 00:57:27.050 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
press him-- really?

00:57:27.050 --> 00:57:28.550 align:middle line:90%
So show me.

00:57:28.550 --> 00:57:30.532 align:middle line:90%
Show me this.

00:57:30.532 --> 00:57:31.490 align:middle line:90%
And then I'll go down--

00:57:31.490 --> 00:57:32.948 align:middle line:84%
show me, show me,
show me, show me.

00:57:32.948 --> 00:57:35.520 align:middle line:84%
And then he's stuck
because it's an input.

00:57:35.520 --> 00:57:36.770 align:middle line:90%
So this is very easy approach.

00:57:36.770 --> 00:57:39.560 align:middle line:84%
The problem with this approach,
I lose probability 1/2 in each

00:57:39.560 --> 00:57:45.380 align:middle line:84%
layer because suppose if it's
an AND, let's say it's a 0,

00:57:45.380 --> 00:57:49.790 align:middle line:84%
then he tells me one
of them is zeroes.

00:57:49.790 --> 00:57:53.360 align:middle line:84%
But in reality, I
know they're both 1.

00:57:53.360 --> 00:57:55.380 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
tell them, really?

00:57:55.380 --> 00:57:56.250 align:middle line:90%
Both of them are 0?

00:57:56.250 --> 00:57:58.430 align:middle line:90%
Fine, I'll choose one of them.

00:57:58.430 --> 00:57:59.610 align:middle line:90%
Show me this is 0.

00:57:59.610 --> 00:58:03.120 align:middle line:84%
But maybe this really was the
0,1, and then he'll, of course,

00:58:03.120 --> 00:58:03.670 align:middle line:90%
cheat.

00:58:03.670 --> 00:58:06.840 align:middle line:84%
So I'm like, finger crossed,
this was actually a 1,

00:58:06.840 --> 00:58:10.350 align:middle line:90%
but only with probability 1/2.

00:58:10.350 --> 00:58:11.790 align:middle line:90%
So that idea works very well.

00:58:11.790 --> 00:58:14.507 align:middle line:84%
It's just the sum is
1 over 2 to the d.

00:58:14.507 --> 00:58:16.340 align:middle line:84%
So that's not good
because 1 over 2 to the d

00:58:16.340 --> 00:58:17.880 align:middle line:90%
is the size of the circuit.

00:58:17.880 --> 00:58:19.830 align:middle line:90%
I need to-- that sucks.

00:58:19.830 --> 00:58:22.670 align:middle line:90%
OK, no good.

00:58:22.670 --> 00:58:24.050 align:middle line:90%
So what do we do?

00:58:24.050 --> 00:58:25.930 align:middle line:90%
So here is the basic idea.

00:58:25.930 --> 00:58:27.620 align:middle line:90%
The idea is, you know what?

00:58:27.620 --> 00:58:30.200 align:middle line:90%
Don't ask per gate, per gate.

00:58:30.200 --> 00:58:31.820 align:middle line:90%
That's not a good idea.

00:58:31.820 --> 00:58:36.220 align:middle line:84%
Let's ask something about an
encoding of the entire layer.

00:58:36.220 --> 00:58:38.500 align:middle line:84%
So instead, I'm going
to have to give me

00:58:38.500 --> 00:58:40.810 align:middle line:84%
something kind of about
the low-degree extension

00:58:40.810 --> 00:58:43.000 align:middle line:90%
of this layer.

00:58:43.000 --> 00:58:44.760 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not going
to ask, reduce it.

00:58:44.760 --> 00:58:45.700 align:middle line:90%
Tell me this.

00:58:45.700 --> 00:58:46.690 align:middle line:90%
Tell me this.

00:58:46.690 --> 00:58:50.080 align:middle line:84%
No, tell me
something about this.

00:58:50.080 --> 00:58:53.560 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to
argue, if he cheated here,

00:58:53.560 --> 00:58:59.010 align:middle line:84%
he must also cheat here
with very high probability.

00:58:59.010 --> 00:59:00.780 align:middle line:84%
And again, if you
cheat here, I'm

00:59:00.780 --> 00:59:03.060 align:middle line:84%
going to argue that
you must cheat here

00:59:03.060 --> 00:59:07.042 align:middle line:84%
with very high probability,
and so on and so forth.

00:59:07.042 --> 00:59:08.500 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of the
high-level idea.

00:59:08.500 --> 00:59:11.440 align:middle line:84%
But now you can say, well,
this is a bit confusing.

00:59:11.440 --> 00:59:14.200 align:middle line:84%
Before, when I said,
if you cheated here,

00:59:14.200 --> 00:59:17.970 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to ask him open
cheat on one of these,

00:59:17.970 --> 00:59:19.410 align:middle line:90%
it's very easy for me to check.

00:59:19.410 --> 00:59:21.720 align:middle line:84%
If you say, this is
0, and it's an AND,

00:59:21.720 --> 00:59:25.170 align:middle line:84%
you're claiming that
one of these is 0.

00:59:25.170 --> 00:59:27.070 align:middle line:90%
But I know one of these is 1.

00:59:27.070 --> 00:59:29.790 align:middle line:90%
So it's very easy to argue.

00:59:29.790 --> 00:59:31.240 align:middle line:90%
How do I go--

00:59:31.240 --> 00:59:35.490 align:middle line:84%
So I can just say, OK, prove
to me that this is a 0.

00:59:35.490 --> 00:59:36.610 align:middle line:90%
That's easy.

00:59:36.610 --> 00:59:42.180 align:middle line:84%
How do I go from arguing
about the low-degree extension

00:59:42.180 --> 00:59:46.080 align:middle line:84%
of a layer to a low-degree
extension of another layer?

00:59:46.080 --> 00:59:47.570 align:middle line:90%
What do I ask you even?

00:59:47.570 --> 00:59:50.100 align:middle line:90%


00:59:50.100 --> 00:59:52.380 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out
that what I do here

00:59:52.380 --> 00:59:56.540 align:middle line:84%
is really low-degree
extension and Sumcheck.

00:59:56.540 --> 00:59:58.320 align:middle line:90%
So I go from here to here.

00:59:58.320 --> 01:00:00.080 align:middle line:90%
It's just a Sumcheck.

01:00:00.080 --> 01:00:02.790 align:middle line:84%
So that's what
we're going to see.

01:00:02.790 --> 01:00:06.590 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of the
high-level idea.

01:00:06.590 --> 01:00:10.100 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

01:00:10.100 --> 01:00:12.456 align:middle line:84%
Let me just make sure I didn't
forget to say something.

01:00:12.456 --> 01:00:15.150 align:middle line:90%


01:00:15.150 --> 01:00:17.270 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

01:00:17.270 --> 01:00:19.940 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: What's the goal
for the verifier runtime?

01:00:19.940 --> 01:00:20.840 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Good, OK.

01:00:20.840 --> 01:00:21.882 align:middle line:90%
What we're going to get--

01:00:21.882 --> 01:00:24.750 align:middle line:84%
OK, goal, as
efficient as possible.

01:00:24.750 --> 01:00:28.230 align:middle line:84%
What we get is the
verifier's runtime

01:00:28.230 --> 01:00:31.970 align:middle line:84%
and the communication
complexity will be d, the depth,

01:00:31.970 --> 01:00:34.210 align:middle line:90%
times polylog the circuit size.

01:00:34.210 --> 01:00:36.890 align:middle line:84%
And the reason we
paid this depth

01:00:36.890 --> 01:00:39.620 align:middle line:84%
is because in the
protocol, we're

01:00:39.620 --> 01:00:41.790 align:middle line:90%
going to run d sub protocols.

01:00:41.790 --> 01:00:46.190 align:middle line:84%
We're going to go from
a claim on layer i

01:00:46.190 --> 01:00:47.450 align:middle line:90%
to another claim of layer i.

01:00:47.450 --> 01:00:53.600 align:middle line:84%
And each kind of
reduction in the layers

01:00:53.600 --> 01:00:56.120 align:middle line:84%
is going to be kind of a
Sumcheck protocol, which

01:00:56.120 --> 01:01:01.100 align:middle line:84%
will consist of log
S rounds or log S--

01:01:01.100 --> 01:01:04.490 align:middle line:84%
the variable's
going to be log S.

01:01:04.490 --> 01:01:07.220 align:middle line:84%
So eventually, the
runtime of the verifier

01:01:07.220 --> 01:01:11.450 align:middle line:84%
will be something like the depth
times log the circuit size.

01:01:11.450 --> 01:01:14.660 align:middle line:84%
And the prover is going to run
time poly in the circuit size.

01:01:14.660 --> 01:01:17.520 align:middle line:84%
But we'll go over
all these parameters

01:01:17.520 --> 01:01:18.730 align:middle line:90%
once we see the protocol.

01:01:18.730 --> 01:01:21.060 align:middle line:84%
But that's kind of
the theorem that we'll

01:01:21.060 --> 01:01:23.130 align:middle line:90%
write when we get there.

01:01:23.130 --> 01:01:23.830 align:middle line:90%
Great.

01:01:23.830 --> 01:01:24.820 align:middle line:90%
Thanks.

01:01:24.820 --> 01:01:26.180 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

01:01:26.180 --> 01:01:26.860 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:01:26.860 --> 01:01:30.010 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So the verifier isn't
able to see the whole circuit.

01:01:30.010 --> 01:01:30.790 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Right.

01:01:30.790 --> 01:01:32.230 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: How does
the verifier get--

01:01:32.230 --> 01:01:34.188 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: Good, good,
good, good, good, good.

01:01:34.188 --> 01:01:37.000 align:middle line:84%
So the verifier, as
I said, he can't even

01:01:37.000 --> 01:01:38.010 align:middle line:90%
see the entire circuit.

01:01:38.010 --> 01:01:41.050 align:middle line:84%
All he has is some
uniform Turing machine

01:01:41.050 --> 01:01:42.450 align:middle line:90%
that generates the circuit.

01:01:42.450 --> 01:01:44.540 align:middle line:90%
So that's a very good question.

01:01:44.540 --> 01:01:49.400 align:middle line:90%
How does he-- we'll see.

01:01:49.400 --> 01:01:52.530 align:middle line:84%
The answer is, it's
complicated, but we'll see.

01:01:52.530 --> 01:01:55.310 align:middle line:90%
We'll see as we go along.

01:01:55.310 --> 01:01:59.430 align:middle line:84%
He'll use his kind of uniform
access to help him out.

01:01:59.430 --> 01:02:02.480 align:middle line:84%
It's really unclear
how at this point.

01:02:02.480 --> 01:02:03.020 align:middle line:90%
Great.

01:02:03.020 --> 01:02:05.260 align:middle line:90%
OK, so what do we do?

01:02:05.260 --> 01:02:11.000 align:middle line:84%
So first thing, so step 1, this
is just, again, for simplicity,

01:02:11.000 --> 01:02:13.430 align:middle line:90%
arithmetize the circuit.

01:02:13.430 --> 01:02:19.030 align:middle line:84%
Remember last time we
had arithmetization?

01:02:19.030 --> 01:02:23.320 align:middle line:84%
So arithmetize, all I mean
here is convert an OR to--

01:02:23.320 --> 01:02:25.570 align:middle line:84%
convert an AND to
multiplication,

01:02:25.570 --> 01:02:28.540 align:middle line:84%
convert an OR into the
sum minus multiplication.

01:02:28.540 --> 01:02:35.350 align:middle line:84%
Just convert the AND/OR gates
into addition and multiplication

01:02:35.350 --> 01:02:36.190 align:middle line:90%
gate.

01:02:36.190 --> 01:02:55.490 align:middle line:84%
So step 1, arithmetize C so
that it consists of only and--

01:02:55.490 --> 01:02:59.865 align:middle line:90%
sorry, add, and multi gates.

01:02:59.865 --> 01:03:03.730 align:middle line:90%


01:03:03.730 --> 01:03:04.580 align:middle line:90%
Now, you're right.

01:03:04.580 --> 01:03:07.510 align:middle line:84%
At this point, the verifier
doesn't even know--

01:03:07.510 --> 01:03:10.630 align:middle line:84%
so we'll talk about
how the verifier has

01:03:10.630 --> 01:03:12.350 align:middle line:90%
access to these things later.

01:03:12.350 --> 01:03:15.320 align:middle line:84%
But the prover, what
he does at least,

01:03:15.320 --> 01:03:17.900 align:middle line:84%
the prover first
arithmetizes the circuit.

01:03:17.900 --> 01:03:22.010 align:middle line:84%
So it's add, and now everything
all the gates are multiplication

01:03:22.010 --> 01:03:24.520 align:middle line:90%
gates and addition gates.

01:03:24.520 --> 01:03:25.770 align:middle line:90%
Good.

01:03:25.770 --> 01:03:29.700 align:middle line:84%
Now, what he does, so I want
to convince you that C of x we

01:03:29.700 --> 01:03:31.590 align:middle line:90%
said 1, so I'll make it 1.

01:03:31.590 --> 01:03:33.270 align:middle line:90%
What do I do?

01:03:33.270 --> 01:03:34.800 align:middle line:90%
I'm the prover.

01:03:34.800 --> 01:03:37.050 align:middle line:90%
I take my input x.

01:03:37.050 --> 01:03:41.990 align:middle line:84%
I compute the values of all
the gates in the circuit.

01:03:41.990 --> 01:03:43.115 align:middle line:90%
So what does the prover do?

01:03:43.115 --> 01:03:46.340 align:middle line:90%


01:03:46.340 --> 01:04:02.110 align:middle line:84%
First, compute the value of
all the gates of C. So now

01:04:02.110 --> 01:04:06.190 align:middle line:84%
the prover computed
all these bits, which

01:04:06.190 --> 01:04:08.290 align:middle line:90%
are the values of the gates.

01:04:08.290 --> 01:04:10.150 align:middle line:90%
Note, this is still over--

01:04:10.150 --> 01:04:10.790 align:middle line:90%
it's Boolean.

01:04:10.790 --> 01:04:13.810 align:middle line:90%
It's over g of 2.

01:04:13.810 --> 01:04:16.090 align:middle line:90%
Everything is 0,1.

01:04:16.090 --> 01:04:16.900 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:04:16.900 --> 01:04:21.640 align:middle line:90%
Next thing I do is--

01:04:21.640 --> 01:04:25.600 align:middle line:84%
the prover does, he computes
the low-degree extension

01:04:25.600 --> 01:04:27.730 align:middle line:90%
of each layer.

01:04:27.730 --> 01:04:30.310 align:middle line:90%
So what are the parameters?

01:04:30.310 --> 01:04:31.400 align:middle line:90%
How do we do it?

01:04:31.400 --> 01:04:32.880 align:middle line:90%
There's many ways to do it.

01:04:32.880 --> 01:04:37.070 align:middle line:90%


01:04:37.070 --> 01:04:40.580 align:middle line:84%
Let me add, actually, another
assumption just for simplicity.

01:04:40.580 --> 01:04:44.830 align:middle line:84%
Let's assume that the number
of gates in each layer

01:04:44.830 --> 01:04:47.880 align:middle line:84%
is exactly S, like the
size of the circuit.

01:04:47.880 --> 01:04:50.380 align:middle line:84%
I'm just assuming that so I
don't need to now have different

01:04:50.380 --> 01:04:50.880 align:middle line:90%
S's.

01:04:50.880 --> 01:04:52.180 align:middle line:90%
It's just going to be annoying.

01:04:52.180 --> 01:04:55.300 align:middle line:84%
Why can I assume that the
number of gates in each layer

01:04:55.300 --> 01:04:56.050 align:middle line:90%
is exactly S?

01:04:56.050 --> 01:04:57.980 align:middle line:90%
Just add dummy gates or just 0.

01:04:57.980 --> 01:04:59.710 align:middle line:90%
Nobody's going to look at them.

01:04:59.710 --> 01:05:00.700 align:middle line:90%
I'm just going to--

01:05:00.700 --> 01:05:03.650 align:middle line:84%
it'll just make
my notations easy.

01:05:03.650 --> 01:05:07.550 align:middle line:84%
So now, if there's less,
just add zeroes, 0, 0, 0, 0.

01:05:07.550 --> 01:05:09.760 align:middle line:84%
So now it's like
this kind of circuit

01:05:09.760 --> 01:05:12.610 align:middle line:84%
by just adding
zeroes everywhere.

01:05:12.610 --> 01:05:13.930 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

01:05:13.930 --> 01:05:17.060 align:middle line:90%
Now, what does the prover do?

01:05:17.060 --> 01:05:30.520 align:middle line:84%
He thinks-- so now, each
layer consists of S values.

01:05:30.520 --> 01:05:33.530 align:middle line:84%
That's an assumption,
that we added demi gates.

01:05:33.530 --> 01:05:36.280 align:middle line:84%
So now we have S
values per layer.

01:05:36.280 --> 01:05:38.810 align:middle line:84%
Many of them can be 0,
if the layer was short,

01:05:38.810 --> 01:05:41.480 align:middle line:90%
but there's S values.

01:05:41.480 --> 01:05:44.560 align:middle line:90%


01:05:44.560 --> 01:05:46.270 align:middle line:90%
So let's write these values.

01:05:46.270 --> 01:05:51.980 align:middle line:84%
This is for layer i, let's write
these values in a hypercube.

01:05:51.980 --> 01:05:55.780 align:middle line:84%
So let's write these values
as kind of a function from H

01:05:55.780 --> 01:06:06.450 align:middle line:84%
to the n to 0,1, where
H to the m is exactly S.

01:06:06.450 --> 01:06:09.210 align:middle line:84%
So we said each
layer has S elements.

01:06:09.210 --> 01:06:12.238 align:middle line:84%
Write them in some hypercube
where the size of the hypercube

01:06:12.238 --> 01:06:14.280 align:middle line:84%
is exactly S, so you can
write all these elements

01:06:14.280 --> 01:06:17.500 align:middle line:84%
in the hypercube in some
order that makes sense to you.

01:06:17.500 --> 01:06:20.830 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to
take H to be 0,1,

01:06:20.830 --> 01:06:23.960 align:middle line:84%
just take the binary
representation of each gate.

01:06:23.960 --> 01:06:28.210 align:middle line:84%
The gates are numbered,
and Vi of this number

01:06:28.210 --> 01:06:32.850 align:middle line:84%
is the value of this
gate in layer i.

01:06:32.850 --> 01:06:37.400 align:middle line:84%
So each gate in layer i
has kind of a number, which

01:06:37.400 --> 01:06:42.780 align:middle line:84%
is a number in H to the m, and
this is the value of the gate.

01:06:42.780 --> 01:06:45.020 align:middle line:90%
Now, what is H and what is m?

01:06:45.020 --> 01:06:48.650 align:middle line:84%
I said it needs to be
of size H to the m.

01:06:48.650 --> 01:06:50.840 align:middle line:84%
H to the m needs to be S
so I have place to write

01:06:50.840 --> 01:06:55.080 align:middle line:90%
all the values of the layers.

01:06:55.080 --> 01:06:57.720 align:middle line:84%
But which H and
which m do I choose?

01:06:57.720 --> 01:06:59.750 align:middle line:84%
And now, actually,
there's freedom.

01:06:59.750 --> 01:07:03.410 align:middle line:84%
So many times, it's
easier to think

01:07:03.410 --> 01:07:06.920 align:middle line:84%
like we're used to binary
because we're kind of computers.

01:07:06.920 --> 01:07:11.300 align:middle line:84%
So it's comfortable to think of
H as being 0,1 and m to be log

01:07:11.300 --> 01:07:14.530 align:middle line:90%
S. You can think of it this way.

01:07:14.530 --> 01:07:15.510 align:middle line:90%
That's fine.

01:07:15.510 --> 01:07:24.310 align:middle line:84%
However, later, like probably
Rachel will tell you about it,

01:07:24.310 --> 01:07:25.950 align:middle line:90%
it'll be convenient, actually--

01:07:25.950 --> 01:07:27.940 align:middle line:84%
Rachel is like, oh no,
what did I sign up for.

01:07:27.940 --> 01:07:30.510 align:middle line:84%
It's like, I'm not
going to tell you.

01:07:30.510 --> 01:07:32.880 align:middle line:84%
It'll be convenient,
actually, to take

01:07:32.880 --> 01:07:44.690 align:middle line:84%
the parameters, which are H
being log S and m being log S--

01:07:44.690 --> 01:07:45.190 align:middle line:90%
fine.

01:07:45.190 --> 01:07:46.607 align:middle line:84%
I tried to put in
the same, didn't

01:07:46.607 --> 01:07:54.920 align:middle line:84%
work-- m being log
S over log log S.

01:07:54.920 --> 01:07:58.050 align:middle line:84%
So it'll be convenient to
think of these parameters.

01:07:58.050 --> 01:08:05.070 align:middle line:84%
So first, just note that
log S to the power of m,

01:08:05.070 --> 01:08:11.240 align:middle line:84%
which is log S over
log log S, is just--

01:08:11.240 --> 01:08:17.374 align:middle line:84%
this is just 2 to the log
log S times this thing.

01:08:17.374 --> 01:08:21.870 align:middle line:90%


01:08:21.870 --> 01:08:24.700 align:middle line:84%
These two cancels, and
therefore you get indeed S.

01:08:24.700 --> 01:08:27.640 align:middle line:84%
So I'm just saying,
these parameters,

01:08:27.640 --> 01:08:30.210 align:middle line:84%
indeed, if you take
H to the power of m,

01:08:30.210 --> 01:08:36.729 align:middle line:84%
you get S back because H
log S to the power of m

01:08:36.729 --> 01:08:41.093 align:middle line:84%
log S over log log S, log S
is like 2 to the log log S,

01:08:41.093 --> 01:08:43.260 align:middle line:84%
and then the two log log S
canceled, and you get two

01:08:43.260 --> 01:08:47.439 align:middle line:84%
to the log S, which
is S. So this is good.

01:08:47.439 --> 01:08:50.080 align:middle line:90%
You get what you want.

01:08:50.080 --> 01:08:52.180 align:middle line:84%
So you can be like,
why am I taking

01:08:52.180 --> 01:08:53.380 align:middle line:90%
this setting of parameters?

01:08:53.380 --> 01:08:54.880 align:middle line:84%
And you also may
be kind of annoyed,

01:08:54.880 --> 01:08:56.870 align:middle line:84%
like, why are we talking
so much about parameters?

01:08:56.870 --> 01:08:57.920 align:middle line:90%
This is kind of boring.

01:08:57.920 --> 01:08:58.870 align:middle line:90%
And I agree.

01:08:58.870 --> 01:09:01.779 align:middle line:84%
However, this is important
because these parameters

01:09:01.779 --> 01:09:04.279 align:middle line:84%
are used a lot and
for a good reason.

01:09:04.279 --> 01:09:07.240 align:middle line:84%
So let me tell you why
these parameters, why am I

01:09:07.240 --> 01:09:09.250 align:middle line:84%
spending your time
on these parameters,

01:09:09.250 --> 01:09:11.100 align:middle line:90%
this specific choice.

01:09:11.100 --> 01:09:14.279 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is
that later, when

01:09:14.279 --> 01:09:16.859 align:middle line:84%
we'll take extension--
remember, we

01:09:16.859 --> 01:09:18.279 align:middle line:90%
take an extension over a field.

01:09:18.279 --> 01:09:19.580 align:middle line:90%
We'll take a big field.

01:09:19.580 --> 01:09:24.490 align:middle line:84%
Now, the field, if you
remember the Sumcheck protocol,

01:09:24.490 --> 01:09:28.380 align:middle line:84%
the soundness you
get is like m times

01:09:28.380 --> 01:09:32.939 align:middle line:84%
d, the number of variables
times the degree over f.

01:09:32.939 --> 01:09:38.399 align:middle line:84%
So f needs to be of
size at least m times d.

01:09:38.399 --> 01:09:43.260 align:middle line:84%
So it will need to be at
least like this m, which is,

01:09:43.260 --> 01:09:47.920 align:middle line:84%
if you take binary, like 0,1,
it'll need to be at least log S,

01:09:47.920 --> 01:09:48.660 align:middle line:90%
which is the m.

01:09:48.660 --> 01:09:52.439 align:middle line:84%
Or here, the number of variables
is not constant usually,

01:09:52.439 --> 01:09:55.590 align:middle line:84%
and it'll need to be bigger
than the number of variables.

01:09:55.590 --> 01:10:01.710 align:middle line:84%
And now the problem
is if you take--

01:10:01.710 --> 01:10:05.760 align:middle line:84%
so what's very desirable
in later applications,

01:10:05.760 --> 01:10:07.573 align:middle line:84%
in particular for
PCP applications,

01:10:07.573 --> 01:10:09.490 align:middle line:84%
which we'll talk about,
it's really beautiful.

01:10:09.490 --> 01:10:11.715 align:middle line:84%
For those who haven't
heard of what PCPs are,

01:10:11.715 --> 01:10:12.590 align:middle line:90%
we'll learn about it.

01:10:12.590 --> 01:10:13.670 align:middle line:90%
It's really, really nice.

01:10:13.670 --> 01:10:19.090 align:middle line:84%
But for these applications,
what you want is that f the size

01:10:19.090 --> 01:10:27.060 align:middle line:84%
of the extension you
take is poly in H.

01:10:27.060 --> 01:10:30.340 align:middle line:84%
And now if you take H to
be 0,1, which is binary,

01:10:30.340 --> 01:10:35.130 align:middle line:84%
which is what we all like, then
the field becomes constant size

01:10:35.130 --> 01:10:37.020 align:middle line:84%
because it needs to
be for applications.

01:10:37.020 --> 01:10:39.940 align:middle line:84%
Like, to get the
PCP, you need f.

01:10:39.940 --> 01:10:43.150 align:middle line:84%
If f is bigger, you'll get
a super polynomial object.

01:10:43.150 --> 01:10:48.810 align:middle line:84%
So for later applications, it's
important that the field is not

01:10:48.810 --> 01:10:53.070 align:middle line:84%
too much bigger than H, that
it's polynomially related to H.

01:10:53.070 --> 01:10:58.090 align:middle line:84%
And then if you take H to be
small, like constant, like 0,1,

01:10:58.090 --> 01:11:00.840 align:middle line:84%
then f is a constant, and then
you don't get sums because m is

01:11:00.840 --> 01:11:01.850 align:middle line:90%
not a constant.

01:11:01.850 --> 01:11:03.100 align:middle line:90%
So you get sums bigger than 1.

01:11:03.100 --> 01:11:04.260 align:middle line:90%
You got nothing.

01:11:04.260 --> 01:11:07.735 align:middle line:84%
So if you didn't follow,
it doesn't matter.

01:11:07.735 --> 01:11:09.360 align:middle line:84%
You will see it when
we talk about PCP.

01:11:09.360 --> 01:11:12.870 align:middle line:84%
But this is just to tell you
why I'm choosing these weird

01:11:12.870 --> 01:11:14.675 align:middle line:90%
parameters as opposed to 0,1.

01:11:14.675 --> 01:11:16.800 align:middle line:84%
The point is that later
we'll need these parameters

01:11:16.800 --> 01:11:18.300 align:middle line:90%
when we'll talk about PCP.

01:11:18.300 --> 01:11:20.610 align:middle line:84%
And so that's why
we're using them now.

01:11:20.610 --> 01:11:22.950 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE] GKR itself?

01:11:22.950 --> 01:11:24.600 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: GKR
itself you don't need.

01:11:24.600 --> 01:11:27.017 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Of course, you're
never writing down the entire--

01:11:27.017 --> 01:11:28.630 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Exactly.

01:11:28.630 --> 01:11:29.130 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

01:11:29.130 --> 01:11:30.670 align:middle line:84%
Because in GKR,
it's interactive.

01:11:30.670 --> 01:11:32.560 align:middle line:84%
You're never writing
down the entire thing.

01:11:32.560 --> 01:11:37.180 align:middle line:84%
For GKR itself, you can take
H to be 0,1 and you can take f

01:11:37.180 --> 01:11:40.470 align:middle line:84%
to be big and the kind
of parameters you want.

01:11:40.470 --> 01:11:42.550 align:middle line:84%
I was actually debating
if to teach it with 0,1,

01:11:42.550 --> 01:11:44.800 align:middle line:84%
and then to change
all the parameters.

01:11:44.800 --> 01:11:48.250 align:middle line:84%
In many-- if you look at
Justin Thaler's notes that are

01:11:48.250 --> 01:11:50.780 align:middle line:84%
in the website, I gave
the exact chapter,

01:11:50.780 --> 01:11:54.580 align:middle line:84%
then he uses 0,1
because it's easier.

01:11:54.580 --> 01:11:57.055 align:middle line:84%
But since I'm going to
change the parameters anyway,

01:11:57.055 --> 01:11:58.430 align:middle line:84%
I thought I'll
just start with m.

01:11:58.430 --> 01:12:00.760 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's good to
get accustomed to these type

01:12:00.760 --> 01:12:03.900 align:middle line:84%
of parameters because
they show up a lot.

01:12:03.900 --> 01:12:06.840 align:middle line:84%
So this is just kind of-- if
you didn't follow exact choice,

01:12:06.840 --> 01:12:07.990 align:middle line:90%
don't worry about it.

01:12:07.990 --> 01:12:10.800 align:middle line:84%
It turns out this is
the choice I want.

01:12:10.800 --> 01:12:15.000 align:middle line:84%
And why, you'll learn about
it when we talk about PCPs.

01:12:15.000 --> 01:12:20.280 align:middle line:84%
But if you want to think
about H as 0,1 and m as log S,

01:12:20.280 --> 01:12:21.360 align:middle line:90%
you can do so.

01:12:21.360 --> 01:12:22.800 align:middle line:84%
All the lecture,
everything I say

01:12:22.800 --> 01:12:26.010 align:middle line:84%
makes sense in that parameters,
and you'll understand everything

01:12:26.010 --> 01:12:28.310 align:middle line:84%
with these parameters
as well for today.

01:12:28.310 --> 01:12:31.590 align:middle line:90%


01:12:31.590 --> 01:12:34.090 align:middle line:90%
So good.

01:12:34.090 --> 01:12:37.270 align:middle line:90%
So now what will the prover do?

01:12:37.270 --> 01:12:46.120 align:middle line:84%
The prover will-- he
has for every layer,

01:12:46.120 --> 01:12:49.090 align:middle line:90%
he has the values of the gates.

01:12:49.090 --> 01:12:54.930 align:middle line:84%
He computes the values of the
gates of that layer, which

01:12:54.930 --> 01:13:02.350 align:middle line:84%
you can think about it
as for each layer i,

01:13:02.350 --> 01:13:07.360 align:middle line:84%
the prover computes Vi,
which is the value of all

01:13:07.360 --> 01:13:09.130 align:middle line:90%
the gates in layer i.

01:13:09.130 --> 01:13:15.680 align:middle line:84%
So we can think of the gates as
going from H to the m to 0,1.

01:13:15.680 --> 01:13:17.950 align:middle line:84%
Each gate has a
label in H to the m

01:13:17.950 --> 01:13:20.470 align:middle line:84%
because it's kind of the
same as it is amorphous to S.

01:13:20.470 --> 01:13:23.540 align:middle line:90%
So each gate has a label here.

01:13:23.540 --> 01:13:29.170 align:middle line:84%
And this is just the value
of the gate on input x.

01:13:29.170 --> 01:13:30.690 align:middle line:90%
So this is the proof.

01:13:30.690 --> 01:13:31.690 align:middle line:90%
There's no protocol yet.

01:13:31.690 --> 01:13:32.760 align:middle line:84%
I'm just telling you
what the prover computes

01:13:32.760 --> 01:13:33.885 align:middle line:90%
before the protocol begins.

01:13:33.885 --> 01:13:36.600 align:middle line:90%


01:13:36.600 --> 01:13:43.860 align:middle line:84%
And now the prover can compute
the low-degree extension.

01:13:43.860 --> 01:13:46.760 align:middle line:84%
So this-- let me actually
be careful with room.

01:13:46.760 --> 01:13:47.710 align:middle line:90%
So I'll write it here.

01:13:47.710 --> 01:13:53.070 align:middle line:90%


01:13:53.070 --> 01:13:57.040 align:middle line:84%
This is nothing but the
low-degree extension of Vi.

01:13:57.040 --> 01:14:01.270 align:middle line:90%


01:14:01.270 --> 01:14:03.590 align:middle line:90%
OK, now we're in business.

01:14:03.590 --> 01:14:07.650 align:middle line:84%
Once the prover computed this,
then we're in good shape.

01:14:07.650 --> 01:14:08.150 align:middle line:90%
So now what?

01:14:08.150 --> 01:14:09.810 align:middle line:84%
Now let's go back
to the protocol.

01:14:09.810 --> 01:14:11.500 align:middle line:90%
What do they do?

01:14:11.500 --> 01:14:16.260 align:middle line:84%
So as we said, the
goal is to reduce.

01:14:16.260 --> 01:14:19.580 align:middle line:84%
So remember, the first thing the
prover gave the verifier-- you

01:14:19.580 --> 01:14:21.110 align:middle line:84%
can think of the
protocol started

01:14:21.110 --> 01:14:26.680 align:middle line:84%
with V0, which is the output
layer, and one element.

01:14:26.680 --> 01:14:28.510 align:middle line:84%
Let me call it Z0,
because we're going

01:14:28.510 --> 01:14:33.700 align:middle line:84%
to call them Z, and Z0 in H
to the m in this time, which

01:14:33.700 --> 01:14:34.510 align:middle line:90%
is kind of--

01:14:34.510 --> 01:14:36.860 align:middle line:84%
actually, the output
layer has just one gate.

01:14:36.860 --> 01:14:37.880 align:middle line:90%
It's 0,1.

01:14:37.880 --> 01:14:41.848 align:middle line:84%
We added a bunch of zeros to
it because dummy gates assume

01:14:41.848 --> 01:14:42.890 align:middle line:90%
that it's kind of size S.

01:14:42.890 --> 01:14:45.340 align:middle line:90%
But there's one gate.

01:14:45.340 --> 01:14:46.940 align:middle line:90%
Let's call this gate Z0.

01:14:46.940 --> 01:14:49.940 align:middle line:84%
And he's arguing on Z0
there's a certain value V0.

01:14:49.940 --> 01:14:51.910 align:middle line:90%
In our case, we said it's 1.

01:14:51.910 --> 01:14:54.160 align:middle line:90%
So he comes with an argument.

01:14:54.160 --> 01:14:57.110 align:middle line:84%
He tells it, look, if you
look at the output layer--

01:14:57.110 --> 01:14:58.215 align:middle line:90%
this is the output layer.

01:14:58.215 --> 01:15:02.390 align:middle line:90%


01:15:02.390 --> 01:15:05.540 align:middle line:84%
If you look at the output
layer and the relevant gate

01:15:05.540 --> 01:15:11.150 align:middle line:84%
and the actual single, not dummy
gate, I claim the output is V0,

01:15:11.150 --> 01:15:12.980 align:middle line:90%
namely, let's say 1.

01:15:12.980 --> 01:15:15.860 align:middle line:84%
Now, I say, why
would I trust you?

01:15:15.860 --> 01:15:18.035 align:middle line:84%
And he said, OK,
let me help you.

01:15:18.035 --> 01:15:18.535 align:middle line:90%
Here.

01:15:18.535 --> 01:15:21.990 align:middle line:90%


01:15:21.990 --> 01:15:24.340 align:middle line:90%
I can always add tilde.

01:15:24.340 --> 01:15:26.100 align:middle line:90%
So now let's see.

01:15:26.100 --> 01:15:31.320 align:middle line:84%
What is V0 tilde
and any element Z?

01:15:31.320 --> 01:15:32.830 align:middle line:90%
What is this value?

01:15:32.830 --> 01:15:34.050 align:middle line:90%
Remember, this is the output.

01:15:34.050 --> 01:15:34.860 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:15:34.860 --> 01:15:38.860 align:middle line:90%
So it is what?

01:15:38.860 --> 01:15:41.340 align:middle line:84%
And what I'm saying here
is actually general.

01:15:41.340 --> 01:15:43.720 align:middle line:90%
Even let's think of i, layer i.

01:15:43.720 --> 01:15:46.310 align:middle line:84%
You can think of layer 0 if
you want, but in any layer.

01:15:46.310 --> 01:15:49.790 align:middle line:84%
Take any layer, and take any
Zi, even in the extension,

01:15:49.790 --> 01:15:51.440 align:middle line:90%
in f to the n.

01:15:51.440 --> 01:15:54.290 align:middle line:84%
Here, it happened to be
that the Z0 was actually

01:15:54.290 --> 01:15:56.180 align:middle line:84%
kind of not in the
extension even because we

01:15:56.180 --> 01:15:57.800 align:middle line:90%
started with a certain gate.

01:15:57.800 --> 01:16:00.440 align:middle line:84%
But let's think of it more
broadly because later we'll

01:16:00.440 --> 01:16:02.630 align:middle line:90%
go to will be in the extension.

01:16:02.630 --> 01:16:08.990 align:middle line:84%
So the prover claims
that this is Vi.

01:16:08.990 --> 01:16:12.790 align:middle line:90%
We start with i equals 0.

01:16:12.790 --> 01:16:14.030 align:middle line:90%
I don't know if that's true.

01:16:14.030 --> 01:16:17.210 align:middle line:84%
I want to reduce it to a
claim about layer i plus 1,

01:16:17.210 --> 01:16:18.250 align:middle line:90%
one layer down.

01:16:18.250 --> 01:16:20.020 align:middle line:90%
How do I do that?

01:16:20.020 --> 01:16:22.400 align:middle line:90%
What is Vi tilde and Zi?

01:16:22.400 --> 01:16:24.860 align:middle line:84%
Zi in general is
in the extension.

01:16:24.860 --> 01:16:27.590 align:middle line:84%
In this case, it's not, but in
general it's in the extension.

01:16:27.590 --> 01:16:30.920 align:middle line:84%
So first thing, let me move
Zi not in the extension.

01:16:30.920 --> 01:16:33.760 align:middle line:84%
So let's remember,
what is Vi tilde?

01:16:33.760 --> 01:16:34.905 align:middle line:90%
Vi tilde is sum.

01:16:34.905 --> 01:16:38.680 align:middle line:90%


01:16:38.680 --> 01:16:43.370 align:middle line:84%
Vi, that's kind of the
original Vi, and H--

01:16:43.370 --> 01:16:45.500 align:middle line:84%
let me make sure I'm
consistent with my notation

01:16:45.500 --> 01:16:46.635 align:middle line:90%
so I won't confuse myself--

01:16:46.635 --> 01:16:51.590 align:middle line:90%


01:16:51.590 --> 01:17:05.900 align:middle line:84%
and point P in H to the
m times this chi P of Zi.

01:17:05.900 --> 01:17:07.310 align:middle line:90%
I just kind of--

01:17:07.310 --> 01:17:09.590 align:middle line:84%
so what is the low-degree
extension of V?

01:17:09.590 --> 01:17:14.200 align:middle line:84%
It's the Vi on every point, not
in the extension, in the cube,

01:17:14.200 --> 01:17:19.010 align:middle line:84%
in H to the m, times this
kind of chi function,

01:17:19.010 --> 01:17:20.550 align:middle line:84%
which was this--
oh, I erased it.

01:17:20.550 --> 01:17:23.390 align:middle line:84%
But it's the-- yeah,
this multiplication.

01:17:23.390 --> 01:17:28.210 align:middle line:84%
Each one of them was this
degree H minus 1 function.

01:17:28.210 --> 01:17:33.310 align:middle line:90%
But what is Vi?

01:17:33.310 --> 01:17:39.370 align:middle line:84%
Vi, we can easily relate
it to the layer one below.

01:17:39.370 --> 01:17:42.940 align:middle line:84%
Vi is either an add gate
or a multiplication gate.

01:17:42.940 --> 01:17:45.743 align:middle line:84%
If it's an add gate, it's an
add of two elements below.

01:17:45.743 --> 01:17:47.160 align:middle line:84%
And if it's a
multiplication, it's

01:17:47.160 --> 01:17:49.470 align:middle line:84%
a multiplication of
two elements below.

01:17:49.470 --> 01:17:53.110 align:middle line:84%
So now, to connect it
to the layer below,

01:17:53.110 --> 01:17:56.220 align:middle line:84%
and this goes to the question
of what is the verifier

01:17:56.220 --> 01:17:57.960 align:middle line:90%
know or doesn't know.

01:17:57.960 --> 01:18:00.810 align:middle line:84%
What I'm going to use
is the circuit has--

01:18:00.810 --> 01:18:03.630 align:middle line:84%
so the circuit is
associated for every i

01:18:03.630 --> 01:18:08.850 align:middle line:84%
with an add function and
the multiplication function.

01:18:08.850 --> 01:18:19.310 align:middle line:84%
And this function goes from 0,1
to the, I guess, 3 log S to 0,1.

01:18:19.310 --> 01:18:24.680 align:middle line:84%
And it just says whether
you take a gate in layer i

01:18:24.680 --> 01:18:28.010 align:middle line:84%
and two gates in layer
i plus 1, and it checks

01:18:28.010 --> 01:18:30.450 align:middle line:90%
whether there's an ad there.

01:18:30.450 --> 01:18:35.220 align:middle line:84%
So it takes kind of
i, j, and k, and it

01:18:35.220 --> 01:18:40.495 align:middle line:90%
says 1 if and only if gate i--

01:18:40.495 --> 01:18:43.500 align:middle line:90%


01:18:43.500 --> 01:18:46.800 align:middle line:90%
sorry, i is taken.

01:18:46.800 --> 01:18:49.410 align:middle line:90%
What notation did I use?

01:18:49.410 --> 01:18:51.820 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:18:51.820 --> 01:18:52.510 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

01:18:52.510 --> 01:19:02.360 align:middle line:84%
It takes a gate P, w1, and
w2 in S, one of the gates,

01:19:02.360 --> 01:19:08.870 align:middle line:84%
and it outputs 1 if and
only if gate P in layer

01:19:08.870 --> 01:19:29.460 align:middle line:84%
i is a add of gates w1 and w2 in
layer i plus 1, and 0 otherwise.

01:19:29.460 --> 01:19:34.500 align:middle line:90%


01:19:34.500 --> 01:19:37.930 align:middle line:90%
I start 0 and I go down to d.

01:19:37.930 --> 01:19:40.400 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, and 0 otherwise.

01:19:40.400 --> 01:19:43.880 align:middle line:90%


01:19:43.880 --> 01:19:47.660 align:middle line:84%
So just check if there's an and
there, if there's an add there.

01:19:47.660 --> 01:19:49.350 align:middle line:90%
And mult is exactly the same.

01:19:49.350 --> 01:19:51.930 align:middle line:84%
Mult checks if there's
a multiplication gate.

01:19:51.930 --> 01:19:56.240 align:middle line:84%
So mult also takes P,
a point P in layer i,

01:19:56.240 --> 01:19:59.180 align:middle line:84%
two points in layer i plus 1,
and it outputs 1 if and only

01:19:59.180 --> 01:20:02.360 align:middle line:84%
if this gate P in
layer i is actually

01:20:02.360 --> 01:20:04.830 align:middle line:84%
a mult gate, a
multiplication gate,

01:20:04.830 --> 01:20:08.770 align:middle line:84%
and it connects to gates
w1w2 in layer ii plus 1.

01:20:08.770 --> 01:20:11.690 align:middle line:90%


01:20:11.690 --> 01:20:12.590 align:middle line:90%
So what is this?

01:20:12.590 --> 01:20:19.680 align:middle line:84%
Now we can say this is
nothing but sum of P.

01:20:19.680 --> 01:20:24.480 align:middle line:84%
Let sum over all the
possible w1 and w2,

01:20:24.480 --> 01:20:27.940 align:middle line:84%
all the gates in the
layer below, and see.

01:20:27.940 --> 01:20:28.930 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it adds.

01:20:28.930 --> 01:20:37.560 align:middle line:84%
So if there's add, if
add ipw1w2, if that's 1,

01:20:37.560 --> 01:20:41.160 align:middle line:90%
then you have Vi.

01:20:41.160 --> 01:20:44.774 align:middle line:90%
Then VP is just Viw1 plus Viw2.

01:20:44.774 --> 01:20:50.110 align:middle line:90%


01:20:50.110 --> 01:20:54.320 align:middle line:90%
Again, if P is an add gate.

01:20:54.320 --> 01:20:56.050 align:middle line:84%
If it's not, an add
gate, you'll get a 0.

01:20:56.050 --> 01:20:57.630 align:middle line:90%
So you're not adding anything.

01:20:57.630 --> 01:21:01.810 align:middle line:84%
If it happens to be an add gate,
then the value of this gate

01:21:01.810 --> 01:21:03.945 align:middle line:84%
is just the sum of the
values of the gates below.

01:21:03.945 --> 01:21:06.590 align:middle line:90%


01:21:06.590 --> 01:21:09.680 align:middle line:84%
If P is not an add
gate, all this is 0.

01:21:09.680 --> 01:21:14.125 align:middle line:84%
I didn't do anything, because I
multiplied by add, which is 0.

01:21:14.125 --> 01:21:14.625 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:21:14.625 --> 01:21:18.650 align:middle line:90%


01:21:18.650 --> 01:21:30.580 align:middle line:84%
plus-- let me just open--
plus mult Ipw1w2 if it's

01:21:30.580 --> 01:21:37.450 align:middle line:84%
a multiplication gate, and let's
say it's connected to w1, w2,

01:21:37.450 --> 01:21:38.550 align:middle line:90%
I'm still the sum.

01:21:38.550 --> 01:21:40.060 align:middle line:90%
Then what do I do then?

01:21:40.060 --> 01:21:42.890 align:middle line:90%
What's the value of VIP--

01:21:42.890 --> 01:21:44.976 align:middle line:90%
I mean, Vi in point P?

01:21:44.976 --> 01:21:49.240 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Vi and point
w1 times [INAUDIBLE].

01:21:49.240 --> 01:21:52.630 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: Exactly,
Vi and point w1 times,

01:21:52.630 --> 01:21:57.670 align:middle line:84%
because it's a multiplication
gate, Vi in w2.

01:21:57.670 --> 01:21:58.990 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, plus 1.

01:21:58.990 --> 01:21:59.600 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

01:21:59.600 --> 01:22:01.180 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

01:22:01.180 --> 01:22:02.960 align:middle line:90%
Yes, great.

01:22:02.960 --> 01:22:03.460 align:middle line:90%
Good.

01:22:03.460 --> 01:22:07.900 align:middle line:90%


01:22:07.900 --> 01:22:15.610 align:middle line:84%
And all of these times this
polynomial Chi P of Zi.

01:22:15.610 --> 01:22:23.735 align:middle line:90%


01:22:23.735 --> 01:22:24.235 align:middle line:90%
Good.

01:22:24.235 --> 01:22:28.020 align:middle line:90%


01:22:28.020 --> 01:22:30.370 align:middle line:90%
So now where are we?

01:22:30.370 --> 01:22:34.890 align:middle line:84%
We want to prove
something about layer i,

01:22:34.890 --> 01:22:38.610 align:middle line:84%
but we managed to
write it as sum

01:22:38.610 --> 01:22:41.700 align:middle line:90%
of elements in layer i plus 1.

01:22:41.700 --> 01:22:42.632 align:middle line:90%
That's great.

01:22:42.632 --> 01:22:43.840 align:middle line:90%
That's really what we wanted.

01:22:43.840 --> 01:22:44.798 align:middle line:90%
We want one layer down.

01:22:44.798 --> 01:22:47.880 align:middle line:90%


01:22:47.880 --> 01:22:57.000 align:middle line:84%
And note, this is really
similar to a Sumcheck.

01:22:57.000 --> 01:22:57.910 align:middle line:90%
What do we have here?

01:22:57.910 --> 01:22:59.520 align:middle line:90%
We want to prove that Vi--

01:22:59.520 --> 01:23:02.940 align:middle line:84%
so I want to tell the
prover, prove to me

01:23:02.940 --> 01:23:06.100 align:middle line:84%
that instead of-- he
claims Vi is this value.

01:23:06.100 --> 01:23:08.040 align:middle line:84%
I'm like, I don't know
what this value is.

01:23:08.040 --> 01:23:10.130 align:middle line:84%
Prove to me that Vi
is equal to the sum.

01:23:10.130 --> 01:23:12.840 align:middle line:90%


01:23:12.840 --> 01:23:15.750 align:middle line:84%
So prove to me that Vi is equal
to something about the layer

01:23:15.750 --> 01:23:18.280 align:middle line:90%
below.

01:23:18.280 --> 01:23:22.424 align:middle line:84%
How does he prove that
something is equal to the sum?

01:23:22.424 --> 01:23:24.790 align:middle line:90%
Sumcheck, right?

01:23:24.790 --> 01:23:27.160 align:middle line:90%
So do the Sumcheck protocol.

01:23:27.160 --> 01:23:29.920 align:middle line:84%
And now we're saying, wait,
but where is the polynomials?

01:23:29.920 --> 01:23:30.842 align:middle line:90%
No problem.

01:23:30.842 --> 01:23:32.050 align:middle line:90%
Everything's over h of the m.

01:23:32.050 --> 01:23:33.290 align:middle line:90%
Look at the extensions.

01:23:33.290 --> 01:23:36.040 align:middle line:90%


01:23:36.040 --> 01:23:37.430 align:middle line:90%
This is already polynomial.

01:23:37.430 --> 01:23:38.000 align:middle line:90%
We don't care about it.

01:23:38.000 --> 01:23:38.500 align:middle line:90%
That's good.

01:23:38.500 --> 01:23:41.410 align:middle line:90%


01:23:41.410 --> 01:23:43.660 align:middle line:90%
Look at the extensions.

01:23:43.660 --> 01:23:49.440 align:middle line:84%
We have a low-degree
extension up here.

01:23:49.440 --> 01:23:52.660 align:middle line:90%
Any polynomial can be extended.

01:23:52.660 --> 01:23:54.640 align:middle line:90%
Look at the extension.

01:23:54.640 --> 01:24:00.930 align:middle line:84%
So now we want to argue
that Vi equals the sum.

01:24:00.930 --> 01:24:06.570 align:middle line:84%
This entire thing-- this
entire thing, I argue,

01:24:06.570 --> 01:24:09.530 align:middle line:90%
is a low-degree polynomial.

01:24:09.530 --> 01:24:11.650 align:middle line:84%
Why is this a
low-degree polynomial?

01:24:11.650 --> 01:24:12.270 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

01:24:12.270 --> 01:24:16.850 align:middle line:84%
First of all, it's a polynomial
over H to the 3m because m here

01:24:16.850 --> 01:24:21.410 align:middle line:84%
and two w's here, so we're
talking about 3m variables.

01:24:21.410 --> 01:24:22.460 align:middle line:90%
But Mm is small.

01:24:22.460 --> 01:24:29.790 align:middle line:84%
This is good. m is log S over
log log S, so that's good.

01:24:29.790 --> 01:24:31.200 align:middle line:90%
What about here?

01:24:31.200 --> 01:24:32.680 align:middle line:84%
So let's first
look at the degree.

01:24:32.680 --> 01:24:36.130 align:middle line:84%
The degree of these things,
it's the low-degree extension.

01:24:36.130 --> 01:24:39.600 align:middle line:84%
It's H minus 1 of each one,
because I'm really taking each

01:24:39.600 --> 01:24:42.690 align:middle line:84%
one is H. Each add,
everything is kind of-- oh,

01:24:42.690 --> 01:24:48.090 align:middle line:84%
add is even better because we
did 0,1, so add is even better.

01:24:48.090 --> 01:24:54.033 align:middle line:90%
You could do-- oh, sorry.

01:24:54.033 --> 01:24:56.800 align:middle line:90%


01:24:56.800 --> 01:24:57.825 align:middle line:90%
Can I take it back?

01:24:57.825 --> 01:25:02.190 align:middle line:90%


01:25:02.190 --> 01:25:08.520 align:middle line:84%
I also wanted to add an
H. Everything is in--

01:25:08.520 --> 01:25:11.380 align:middle line:84%
I made a mistake of putting 0,1,
but I need to be consistent.

01:25:11.380 --> 01:25:12.840 align:middle line:90%
If I'm in, I'm in H.

01:25:12.840 --> 01:25:18.370 align:middle line:84%
So everything is in
H. So everything here

01:25:18.370 --> 01:25:21.230 align:middle line:90%
is degree H minus 1, so good.

01:25:21.230 --> 01:25:23.440 align:middle line:84%
So it's more than H1
is 1 because I have

01:25:23.440 --> 01:25:26.920 align:middle line:84%
your multiplication. w1
appears here, w1 appears here,

01:25:26.920 --> 01:25:29.180 align:middle line:84%
so it's going to be
degree 2H minus 1.

01:25:29.180 --> 01:25:29.680 align:middle line:90%
Fine.

01:25:29.680 --> 01:25:30.860 align:middle line:90%
But H minus 1 is small.

01:25:30.860 --> 01:25:32.360 align:middle line:90%
It's like log, so two logs.

01:25:32.360 --> 01:25:34.610 align:middle line:84%
So the degree is small, the
number of variables small.

01:25:34.610 --> 01:25:35.680 align:middle line:90%
Perfect.

01:25:35.680 --> 01:25:37.250 align:middle line:90%
Just do Sumcheck.

01:25:37.250 --> 01:25:40.450 align:middle line:84%
So now you're asking,
what is the GKR protocol?

01:25:40.450 --> 01:25:41.880 align:middle line:90%
Sumcheck.

01:25:41.880 --> 01:25:43.350 align:middle line:90%
That's it.

01:25:43.350 --> 01:25:46.720 align:middle line:90%
You start with V0.

01:25:46.720 --> 01:25:47.830 align:middle line:90%
You tell him, do Sumcheck.

01:25:47.830 --> 01:25:51.220 align:middle line:90%


01:25:51.220 --> 01:25:54.290 align:middle line:84%
Convert this claim
about the output,

01:25:54.290 --> 01:25:55.600 align:middle line:90%
which the verifier has no idea.

01:25:55.600 --> 01:25:56.930 align:middle line:90%
It's so far away from him.

01:25:56.930 --> 01:25:59.030 align:middle line:90%
He doesn't know what--

01:25:59.030 --> 01:26:04.110 align:middle line:84%
let's do the Sumcheck protocol
to reduce this claim to a claim

01:26:04.110 --> 01:26:05.280 align:middle line:90%
about--

01:26:05.280 --> 01:26:09.240 align:middle line:90%
about this thing, essentially.

01:26:09.240 --> 01:26:11.760 align:middle line:84%
But now this is very confusing
because what happens when

01:26:11.760 --> 01:26:13.240 align:middle line:90%
we do the Sumcheck protocol?

01:26:13.240 --> 01:26:15.300 align:middle line:84%
When we do the
Sumcheck protocol,

01:26:15.300 --> 01:26:18.660 align:middle line:84%
we reduce, if you remember--
let's look for a second

01:26:18.660 --> 01:26:20.280 align:middle line:90%
at the Sumcheck here.

01:26:20.280 --> 01:26:25.720 align:middle line:84%
We want to prove that the
sum of something there,

01:26:25.720 --> 01:26:26.470 align:middle line:90%
it's a huge thing.

01:26:26.470 --> 01:26:32.640 align:middle line:84%
Add V plus mult, multiplication,
all this craziness, whatever.

01:26:32.640 --> 01:26:35.100 align:middle line:84%
Low degree is equal to
beta, is equal to something,

01:26:35.100 --> 01:26:37.170 align:middle line:90%
in that case, V0.

01:26:37.170 --> 01:26:39.070 align:middle line:90%
We run the Sumcheck protocol.

01:26:39.070 --> 01:26:43.380 align:middle line:84%
At the end, the verifier
needs to compute to check.

01:26:43.380 --> 01:26:45.090 align:middle line:84%
In the Sumcheck
protocol, we assume

01:26:45.090 --> 01:26:50.300 align:middle line:84%
that the verifier has oracle
access to the polynomial.

01:26:50.300 --> 01:26:53.340 align:middle line:84%
But essentially, he needs
to compute this function,

01:26:53.340 --> 01:26:56.240 align:middle line:90%
the polynomial, at some point.

01:26:56.240 --> 01:26:58.380 align:middle line:84%
Now, maybe he has oracle
access at that point.

01:26:58.380 --> 01:27:00.530 align:middle line:90%
Great but maybe.

01:27:00.530 --> 01:27:04.410 align:middle line:84%
But there's no oracles
in this, in GKR.

01:27:04.410 --> 01:27:06.110 align:middle line:90%
So what do we do now?

01:27:06.110 --> 01:27:08.510 align:middle line:84%
But here's an important
observation that we'll use.

01:27:08.510 --> 01:27:11.930 align:middle line:90%
Note, yes, the prover needs--

01:27:11.930 --> 01:27:13.370 align:middle line:90%
sorry, the verifier.

01:27:13.370 --> 01:27:14.960 align:middle line:84%
When he verifies
the Sumcheck, he

01:27:14.960 --> 01:27:19.970 align:middle line:84%
needs to compute the
polynomial on a single point.

01:27:19.970 --> 01:27:25.700 align:middle line:84%
But this point t, t1 of
the tm, is a random point

01:27:25.700 --> 01:27:29.270 align:middle line:90%
that's chosen by the verifier.

01:27:29.270 --> 01:27:30.660 align:middle line:90%
What is the Sumcheck protocol?

01:27:30.660 --> 01:27:33.120 align:middle line:84%
The verifier
chooses a random t1.

01:27:33.120 --> 01:27:36.290 align:middle line:84%
He gets a polynomial,
then chooses a random t2,

01:27:36.290 --> 01:27:37.440 align:middle line:90%
and so on and so forth.

01:27:37.440 --> 01:27:40.370 align:middle line:84%
At the end, he needs to
evaluate f and t1 of the tm

01:27:40.370 --> 01:27:42.800 align:middle line:90%
that he chose.

01:27:42.800 --> 01:27:45.480 align:middle line:90%
We will use this.

01:27:45.480 --> 01:27:47.120 align:middle line:90%
So now let's see.

01:27:47.120 --> 01:27:49.440 align:middle line:90%
What happened here?

01:27:49.440 --> 01:27:55.560 align:middle line:84%
After we did the Sumcheck, the
poor verifier needs to compute--

01:27:55.560 --> 01:27:59.650 align:middle line:84%
let me again, make sure I'm
consistent with my notation.

01:27:59.650 --> 01:28:00.610 align:middle line:90%
Good.

01:28:00.610 --> 01:28:03.860 align:middle line:84%
The verifier now, so
he does the Sumcheck.

01:28:03.860 --> 01:28:07.690 align:middle line:84%
Let's denote kind
of the randomness

01:28:07.690 --> 01:28:13.240 align:middle line:90%
that he used in Sumcheck by a--

01:28:13.240 --> 01:28:14.950 align:middle line:90%
so we started with i.

01:28:14.950 --> 01:28:18.640 align:middle line:84%
Let's say that when we
reached here, let's say,

01:28:18.640 --> 01:28:29.140 align:middle line:84%
he needs to compute a add tilde
on Zi plus 1, because we moved

01:28:29.140 --> 01:28:44.760 align:middle line:84%
to layer i plus 1, 0 Zi plus 1,
1, Zi plus 1, 2 times Vi plus 1,

01:28:44.760 --> 01:28:45.550 align:middle line:90%
and another--

01:28:45.550 --> 01:28:46.990 align:middle line:90%
and so that was one.

01:28:46.990 --> 01:28:50.110 align:middle line:90%
So Z-- don't like this notation.

01:28:50.110 --> 01:28:50.610 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

01:28:50.610 --> 01:28:51.610 align:middle line:90%
Let me change it.

01:28:51.610 --> 01:28:54.610 align:middle line:90%


01:28:54.610 --> 01:28:56.830 align:middle line:84%
It's more cumbersome on
the board than in the--

01:28:56.830 --> 01:28:57.490 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:28:57.490 --> 01:28:59.780 align:middle line:90%
Let me call Z0, Z1, Z2.

01:28:59.780 --> 01:29:01.960 align:middle line:84%
So suppose they do
a Sumcheck protocol.

01:29:01.960 --> 01:29:05.010 align:middle line:84%
In the Sumcheck, it's
on three n variables.

01:29:05.010 --> 01:29:07.320 align:middle line:84%
So let's denote
the randomness used

01:29:07.320 --> 01:29:10.690 align:middle line:84%
by the verifier and the
protocol by Z0, Z1, Z2.

01:29:10.690 --> 01:29:13.093 align:middle line:90%
Each of them is in f to the n.

01:29:13.093 --> 01:29:16.480 align:middle line:90%


01:29:16.480 --> 01:29:19.900 align:middle line:84%
So now the verifier needs
to compute this function

01:29:19.900 --> 01:29:21.970 align:middle line:90%
and the points Z0, Z1, Z2.

01:29:21.970 --> 01:29:23.720 align:middle line:84%
What is this function
at this point?

01:29:23.720 --> 01:29:32.000 align:middle line:84%
It's add times Vi plus
1 tilde on Z1 plus Vi

01:29:32.000 --> 01:29:46.230 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 tilde on Z2 plus
mult Z0, Z1, Z2 times Vi

01:29:46.230 --> 01:29:53.010 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 Z1 tilde times
Vi plus 1 Z2 tilde,

01:29:53.010 --> 01:30:02.210 align:middle line:84%
and then all this
times this chi.

01:30:02.210 --> 01:30:08.440 align:middle line:84%
I'll put it here, chi
p of Z of-- oh, oops.

01:30:08.440 --> 01:30:12.420 align:middle line:90%


01:30:12.420 --> 01:30:15.120 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, I messed this notation.

01:30:15.120 --> 01:30:16.530 align:middle line:90%
This is the Zi.

01:30:16.530 --> 01:30:23.520 align:middle line:90%
OK, sorry.

01:30:23.520 --> 01:30:26.410 align:middle line:84%
Zi is the original point
that we started with.

01:30:26.410 --> 01:30:30.610 align:middle line:84%
We started a claim about
Vi equals Vi tilde of Zi.

01:30:30.610 --> 01:30:33.930 align:middle line:84%
Zi is a fixed point
that we started with.

01:30:33.930 --> 01:30:37.140 align:middle line:84%
The randomness of the
verifier in the subject

01:30:37.140 --> 01:30:39.940 align:middle line:90%
protocol I denote by Z0 Z1, Z2.

01:30:39.940 --> 01:30:41.500 align:middle line:84%
These are chosen
by the verifier.

01:30:41.500 --> 01:30:42.320 align:middle line:90%
Yes, Vinod?

01:30:42.320 --> 01:30:43.860 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: The V
should be Z0, right?

01:30:43.860 --> 01:30:44.710 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

01:30:44.710 --> 01:30:45.440 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: B?

01:30:45.440 --> 01:30:48.707 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

01:30:48.707 --> 01:30:49.790 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah, exactly.

01:30:49.790 --> 01:30:50.708 align:middle line:90%
But I'm in layer i.

01:30:50.708 --> 01:30:51.250 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

01:30:51.250 --> 01:30:53.070 align:middle line:90%
In layer 0, it's just Z0.

01:30:53.070 --> 01:30:54.070 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: No, no, no, no.

01:30:54.070 --> 01:30:58.160 align:middle line:84%
The V, the V, you replace
it with Z0 in this notation.

01:30:58.160 --> 01:30:58.872 align:middle line:90%
