01:39:54.877 --> 01:39:56.960 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Is it going to
be a ratio of polynomials?

01:39:56.960 --> 01:39:58.085 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: No, no, no, no.

01:39:58.085 --> 01:39:58.870 align:middle line:90%
No, no, no, no.

01:39:58.870 --> 01:40:03.440 align:middle line:90%
So what is exactly chi 1?

01:40:03.440 --> 01:40:06.440 align:middle line:84%
How did it-- chi
is well-defined.

01:40:06.440 --> 01:40:09.430 align:middle line:90%
It's just a product of chi 1s.

01:40:09.430 --> 01:40:12.200 align:middle line:90%
Now you can ask, what is chi 1?

01:40:12.200 --> 01:40:13.960 align:middle line:84%
If you understand what
chi 1 is, then you

01:40:13.960 --> 01:40:16.418 align:middle line:84%
understand what chi is, because
chi is just multiplication.

01:40:16.418 --> 01:40:17.390 align:middle line:90%
What is chi 1?

01:40:17.390 --> 01:40:19.220 align:middle line:90%
Chi 1 is a bivariate polynomial.

01:40:19.220 --> 01:40:23.950 align:middle line:90%
It's defined over f 2m to f.

01:40:23.950 --> 01:40:26.000 align:middle line:84%
And so now you're asking,
how is it defined?

01:40:26.000 --> 01:40:28.370 align:middle line:84%
Let me tell you how
it's defined an H first.

01:40:28.370 --> 01:40:30.280 align:middle line:90%
So if both of them--

01:40:30.280 --> 01:40:31.710 align:middle line:90%
first of all, it's a polynomial.

01:40:31.710 --> 01:40:32.960 align:middle line:90%
It's a two-variate polynomial.

01:40:32.960 --> 01:40:34.690 align:middle line:84%
So it's a polynomial
and two variables,

01:40:34.690 --> 01:40:38.240 align:middle line:90%
and both its degree H minus 1.

01:40:38.240 --> 01:40:42.390 align:middle line:84%
Now you can say, how
is it defined on H?

01:40:42.390 --> 01:40:44.060 align:middle line:90%
On H, I'll tell you.

01:40:44.060 --> 01:40:46.808 align:middle line:84%
It's 1 if and only
if they're equal.

01:40:46.808 --> 01:40:48.350 align:middle line:84%
That's how it's
defined, 0 otherwise.

01:40:48.350 --> 01:40:50.480 align:middle line:90%
So I defined it on H.

01:40:50.480 --> 01:40:53.480 align:middle line:84%
Now you can ask, how
do I define on f?

01:40:53.480 --> 01:40:58.280 align:middle line:84%
So on f, I'm going to define
it in the unique way that

01:40:58.280 --> 01:41:01.465 align:middle line:84%
extends it on H. That's how
it's going to be defined.

01:41:01.465 --> 01:41:05.980 align:middle line:90%


01:41:05.980 --> 01:41:09.640 align:middle line:84%
So chi is a
low-degree polynomial

01:41:09.640 --> 01:41:11.440 align:middle line:90%
in each and every variable.

01:41:11.440 --> 01:41:15.160 align:middle line:84%
Before, I wrote it
only on Z. But that

01:41:15.160 --> 01:41:19.450 align:middle line:84%
wasn't good because I need it
also to be low degree on H. So--

01:41:19.450 --> 01:41:20.403 align:middle line:90%
yes, Vinod?

01:41:20.403 --> 01:41:21.820 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Just the
point, the point

01:41:21.820 --> 01:41:24.770 align:middle line:84%
is that the way it was
written down previously

01:41:24.770 --> 01:41:26.710 align:middle line:90%
had H in the denominator.

01:41:26.710 --> 01:41:27.830 align:middle line:90%
It's not a polynomial.

01:41:27.830 --> 01:41:28.170 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Exactly.

01:41:28.170 --> 01:41:29.962 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]
it's not a polynomial.

01:41:29.962 --> 01:41:30.830 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Exactly.

01:41:30.830 --> 01:41:31.910 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: And this one is.

01:41:31.910 --> 01:41:34.245 align:middle line:90%


01:41:34.245 --> 01:41:34.870 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: OK.

01:41:34.870 --> 01:41:36.040 align:middle line:90%
So I write it--

01:41:36.040 --> 01:41:39.440 align:middle line:84%
what's important to me is
that chi 1 is a polynomial.

01:41:39.440 --> 01:41:40.790 align:middle line:90%
It's a two-variate polynomial.

01:41:40.790 --> 01:41:43.820 align:middle line:84%
Both of them are
degree H minus 1.

01:41:43.820 --> 01:41:46.300 align:middle line:90%
So with that, if we believe--

01:41:46.300 --> 01:41:47.990 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

01:41:47.990 --> 01:41:49.782 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: If they're
not equal, is it 0?

01:41:49.782 --> 01:41:50.490 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah.

01:41:50.490 --> 01:41:51.500 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:41:51.500 --> 01:41:53.750 align:middle line:90%
So if H and Z are equal, it's 1.

01:41:53.750 --> 01:41:55.090 align:middle line:90%
If they're not equal, it's 0.

01:41:55.090 --> 01:41:57.480 align:middle line:90%
Yes, great.

01:41:57.480 --> 01:41:58.200 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

01:41:58.200 --> 01:41:59.080 align:middle line:90%
Thank you, guys.

01:41:59.080 --> 01:42:01.680 align:middle line:90%
You're great.

01:42:01.680 --> 01:42:02.850 align:middle line:90%
Fantastic.

01:42:02.850 --> 01:42:08.430 align:middle line:84%
So now we reduce
this big sum to this.

01:42:08.430 --> 01:42:13.322 align:middle line:84%
Because we sum over the P,
so the P becomes Z0, this.

01:42:13.322 --> 01:42:16.120 align:middle line:90%


01:42:16.120 --> 01:42:18.300 align:middle line:90%
Wow, what's going on here?

01:42:18.300 --> 01:42:19.800 align:middle line:84%
What is the verifier
supposed to do?

01:42:19.800 --> 01:42:27.210 align:middle line:84%
So before, he need to verify
one little point in Vi tilde,

01:42:27.210 --> 01:42:29.000 align:middle line:84%
and now he needs to
verify this mess.

01:42:29.000 --> 01:42:35.930 align:middle line:90%


01:42:35.930 --> 01:42:39.150 align:middle line:90%
But let's deconstruct.

01:42:39.150 --> 01:42:39.650 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:42:39.650 --> 01:42:40.730 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Sorry.

01:42:40.730 --> 01:42:45.100 align:middle line:84%
Is the argument [INAUDIBLE]
chi [INAUDIBLE] Z1 or Zi?

01:42:45.100 --> 01:42:46.810 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: This is good.

01:42:46.810 --> 01:42:49.970 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, this is Zi.

01:42:49.970 --> 01:42:51.870 align:middle line:84%
This is the claim
we started with.

01:42:51.870 --> 01:42:59.110 align:middle line:84%
We started with a
claim on layer i.

01:42:59.110 --> 01:43:01.070 align:middle line:84%
You can think of i as
0 in the beginning.

01:43:01.070 --> 01:43:02.830 align:middle line:84%
But we start with
the claim that Vi

01:43:02.830 --> 01:43:06.700 align:middle line:84%
is equal the
low-degree extension

01:43:06.700 --> 01:43:08.830 align:middle line:90%
of layer i in point Zi.

01:43:08.830 --> 01:43:11.960 align:middle line:90%
This is the Zi here because--

01:43:11.960 --> 01:43:13.130 align:middle line:90%
why is it the Zi?

01:43:13.130 --> 01:43:13.790 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

01:43:13.790 --> 01:43:14.720 align:middle line:90%
What did we say?

01:43:14.720 --> 01:43:18.820 align:middle line:84%
We said that Vi, we need
to argue that it's this.

01:43:18.820 --> 01:43:21.370 align:middle line:84%
But this is just sum--
that's the definition

01:43:21.370 --> 01:43:24.670 align:middle line:84%
of a low-degree extension,
sum of the points times this.

01:43:24.670 --> 01:43:28.600 align:middle line:90%
Let me change this to P Zi.

01:43:28.600 --> 01:43:29.737 align:middle line:90%
The Zi I is fixed.

01:43:29.737 --> 01:43:30.320 align:middle line:90%
This is fixed.

01:43:30.320 --> 01:43:33.460 align:middle line:90%
This is from the claim.

01:43:33.460 --> 01:43:34.190 align:middle line:90%
Good.

01:43:34.190 --> 01:43:38.560 align:middle line:84%
Now we say let's sum over the
P, so we sum over the P. The P

01:43:38.560 --> 01:43:39.170 align:middle line:90%
is not fixed.

01:43:39.170 --> 01:43:40.180 align:middle line:90%
It's summed over.

01:43:40.180 --> 01:43:43.390 align:middle line:84%
And now we say, let's
instead of this, let's sum

01:43:43.390 --> 01:43:46.340 align:middle line:84%
over the two w's, which
are the possible kids.

01:43:46.340 --> 01:43:48.565 align:middle line:90%
All the possible pair of kids--

01:43:48.565 --> 01:43:50.020 align:middle line:90%
oh, I think I didn't say.

01:43:50.020 --> 01:43:53.980 align:middle line:84%
I assume that C is fan in
two, that each gate has

01:43:53.980 --> 01:43:54.820 align:middle line:90%
only two children.

01:43:54.820 --> 01:43:56.680 align:middle line:84%
And that's, again, with
all this generality,

01:43:56.680 --> 01:44:01.000 align:middle line:84%
you just increase the
depth by log factor.

01:44:01.000 --> 01:44:07.180 align:middle line:84%
So I'm assuming fan in
two, so it's only two.

01:44:07.180 --> 01:44:13.510 align:middle line:84%
Each gate has either an addition
of two or multiplication of two.

01:44:13.510 --> 01:44:15.130 align:middle line:90%
OK, so this is what we get.

01:44:15.130 --> 01:44:21.080 align:middle line:84%
And now when we do the Sumcheck
protocol, the P and the--

01:44:21.080 --> 01:44:24.970 align:middle line:90%
so we sum over P, w1 and w2.

01:44:24.970 --> 01:44:27.567 align:middle line:84%
At the end of the day, we need
to compute this polynomial.

01:44:27.567 --> 01:44:29.650 align:middle line:84%
The verifier will need to
compute this polynomial.

01:44:29.650 --> 01:44:35.200 align:middle line:84%
Instead of over some fixed
P w1, w2, it's over some--

01:44:35.200 --> 01:44:40.583 align:middle line:84%
I called it Z0, Z1, Z2
chosen by the verifier.

01:44:40.583 --> 01:44:42.500 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of the
randomness of the verifier.

01:44:42.500 --> 01:44:45.020 align:middle line:90%
So P becomes Z0.

01:44:45.020 --> 01:44:48.150 align:middle line:90%
w1 becomes Z1, and this is Z2.

01:44:48.150 --> 01:44:49.900 align:middle line:90%
That's kind of the after.

01:44:49.900 --> 01:44:50.550 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:44:50.550 --> 01:44:52.217 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I'm very
confused because then,

01:44:52.217 --> 01:44:55.270 align:middle line:90%
if i is equal to 1 [INAUDIBLE]?

01:44:55.270 --> 01:44:56.872 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: I know, I know.

01:44:56.872 --> 01:44:58.330 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So that's
the different Z.

01:44:58.330 --> 01:44:59.372 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Yeah, I know.

01:44:59.372 --> 01:45:00.130 align:middle line:90%
I'm so sorry.

01:45:00.130 --> 01:45:01.783 align:middle line:90%
These are stars.

01:45:01.783 --> 01:45:03.700 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: OK, now, that
makes a lot more sense.

01:45:03.700 --> 01:45:04.780 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: It's a constant.

01:45:04.780 --> 01:45:06.070 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: It's a constant.

01:45:06.070 --> 01:45:07.680 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, I know that.

01:45:07.680 --> 01:45:11.525 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, that's a bad
notation on my end.

01:45:11.525 --> 01:45:12.025 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:45:12.025 --> 01:45:15.190 align:middle line:90%


01:45:15.190 --> 01:45:17.200 align:middle line:84%
This is a very,
very different Z.

01:45:17.200 --> 01:45:20.410 align:middle line:84%
It's like a totally
different animal.

01:45:20.410 --> 01:45:24.110 align:middle line:84%
These are random Z's chosen
by the honest verifier.

01:45:24.110 --> 01:45:27.247 align:middle line:84%
This is kind of, I
don't know, a Z star

01:45:27.247 --> 01:45:28.455 align:middle line:90%
that was fixed ahead of time.

01:45:28.455 --> 01:45:31.770 align:middle line:90%


01:45:31.770 --> 01:45:35.700 align:middle line:84%
So now, the verifier
needs to verify this.

01:45:35.700 --> 01:45:36.280 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:45:36.280 --> 01:45:39.210 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE] is fixed.

01:45:39.210 --> 01:45:42.480 align:middle line:84%
Can you just use the
same chi [INAUDIBLE]

01:45:42.480 --> 01:45:44.968 align:middle line:84%
before and put it in the
subscript of the chi?

01:45:44.968 --> 01:45:47.010 align:middle line:84%
So then you don't need
the extension or whatever.

01:45:47.010 --> 01:45:50.500 align:middle line:84%
You can just write
it out [INAUDIBLE].

01:45:50.500 --> 01:45:53.710 align:middle line:84%
YAEL KALAI: The thing is that
before, I assumed that the chi,

01:45:53.710 --> 01:45:57.280 align:middle line:84%
when I fixed the H,
it was an H to the m.

01:45:57.280 --> 01:45:58.760 align:middle line:90%
This isn't the extension.

01:45:58.760 --> 01:46:00.610 align:middle line:84%
It's not going to
be an H to the m.

01:46:00.610 --> 01:46:05.320 align:middle line:84%
So I think we should
keep the two, yeah.

01:46:05.320 --> 01:46:10.720 align:middle line:90%
But that's a good idea.

01:46:10.720 --> 01:46:12.250 align:middle line:90%
OK, so what is this?

01:46:12.250 --> 01:46:14.757 align:middle line:84%
So it seems like we had
something that looked really

01:46:14.757 --> 01:46:16.840 align:middle line:84%
kind of reasonable, and
we ended up with something

01:46:16.840 --> 01:46:18.050 align:middle line:90%
that's horrifying.

01:46:18.050 --> 01:46:21.550 align:middle line:84%
So what does the
verifier do now?

01:46:21.550 --> 01:46:23.500 align:middle line:84%
Now the verifier is
like, I just need

01:46:23.500 --> 01:46:27.000 align:middle line:84%
to verify that this equals
some value that we got out

01:46:27.000 --> 01:46:28.250 align:middle line:90%
of the Sumcheck protocol.

01:46:28.250 --> 01:46:31.913 align:middle line:84%
So we did the Sumcheck protocol,
we got some value, whatever.

01:46:31.913 --> 01:46:33.080 align:middle line:90%
I don't know what to denote.

01:46:33.080 --> 01:46:34.497 align:middle line:84%
I'm now scared to
denote anything.

01:46:34.497 --> 01:46:35.900 align:middle line:90%
So this equals something.

01:46:35.900 --> 01:46:37.192 align:middle line:90%
Now, what is he supposed to do?

01:46:37.192 --> 01:46:39.330 align:middle line:90%
He's supposed to verify that.

01:46:39.330 --> 01:46:41.030 align:middle line:90%
What do you do?

01:46:41.030 --> 01:46:44.780 align:middle line:84%
So here is the idea, and
then we'll take a break.

01:46:44.780 --> 01:46:47.280 align:middle line:84%
So he needs to verify
a lot of things.

01:46:47.280 --> 01:46:50.760 align:middle line:84%
First, he needs to compute
this add tilde and mult tilde.

01:46:50.760 --> 01:46:52.480 align:middle line:84%
This goes back to
your question now.

01:46:52.480 --> 01:46:55.443 align:middle line:84%
Add tilde, these are a
function of the circuit.

01:46:55.443 --> 01:46:56.610 align:middle line:90%
He doesn't have the circuit.

01:46:56.610 --> 01:47:00.440 align:middle line:84%
How does he compute these
add tilde and mult tilde?

01:47:00.440 --> 01:47:04.610 align:middle line:84%
Turns out, actually,
this is sad news.

01:47:04.610 --> 01:47:06.160 align:middle line:90%
He actually can't compute.

01:47:06.160 --> 01:47:07.630 align:middle line:90%
We don't know how to compute.

01:47:07.630 --> 01:47:10.580 align:middle line:84%
He doesn't know how to compute
add tilde and mult tilde.

01:47:10.580 --> 01:47:13.180 align:middle line:90%


01:47:13.180 --> 01:47:17.200 align:middle line:84%
Even if he gets this kind
of uniform description

01:47:17.200 --> 01:47:21.020 align:middle line:84%
of the Turing machine that
generates the circuit,

01:47:21.020 --> 01:47:23.390 align:middle line:90%
he can't compute them.

01:47:23.390 --> 01:47:24.900 align:middle line:90%
Let's deal with that later.

01:47:24.900 --> 01:47:26.600 align:middle line:90%
Suppose he has oracle access.

01:47:26.600 --> 01:47:29.630 align:middle line:84%
Suppose someone--
there's a trusted oracle

01:47:29.630 --> 01:47:33.020 align:middle line:84%
that lets him compute
these values for now.

01:47:33.020 --> 01:47:36.540 align:middle line:84%
In the paper, it's called
the bare bones protocol.

01:47:36.540 --> 01:47:38.390 align:middle line:84%
So suppose somehow,
magically he has

01:47:38.390 --> 01:47:40.380 align:middle line:84%
a trusted oracle,
but only to compute

01:47:40.380 --> 01:47:41.660 align:middle line:90%
add tilde and mult tilde.

01:47:41.660 --> 01:47:43.950 align:middle line:84%
Note, these are only
functions of the circuit.

01:47:43.950 --> 01:47:47.780 align:middle line:84%
They have nothing to do with
the claim of the prover.

01:47:47.780 --> 01:47:49.830 align:middle line:84%
It's just information
about the circuit.

01:47:49.830 --> 01:47:51.215 align:middle line:90%
It's whether kind of--

01:47:51.215 --> 01:47:54.620 align:middle line:90%


01:47:54.620 --> 01:47:57.500 align:middle line:84%
not in the extension,
it's whether this gate

01:47:57.500 --> 01:48:00.500 align:middle line:84%
is connected by an AND to
these two gates, yes or no,

01:48:00.500 --> 01:48:03.870 align:middle line:84%
or by a multiplication to
these two gates, yes or no.

01:48:03.870 --> 01:48:05.910 align:middle line:84%
So it's not enough for
him to compute that.

01:48:05.910 --> 01:48:08.310 align:middle line:84%
He needs to compute the
low-degree extension of that.

01:48:08.310 --> 01:48:10.102 align:middle line:84%
But this has nothing
to do with the prover.

01:48:10.102 --> 01:48:12.660 align:middle line:84%
This is just a function
of the circuit.

01:48:12.660 --> 01:48:14.700 align:middle line:84%
So suppose first--
so first, we're

01:48:14.700 --> 01:48:18.220 align:middle line:84%
going to assume this is not
without loss of generality.

01:48:18.220 --> 01:48:20.220 align:middle line:84%
But just for
simplicity, we're going

01:48:20.220 --> 01:48:24.935 align:middle line:90%
to assume that we can compute.

01:48:24.935 --> 01:48:29.296 align:middle line:90%


01:48:29.296 --> 01:48:30.767 align:middle line:90%
add tilde and mult tilde.

01:48:30.767 --> 01:48:36.611 align:middle line:90%


01:48:36.611 --> 01:48:39.277 align:middle line:90%
Suppose he can.

01:48:39.277 --> 01:48:40.860 align:middle line:84%
Actually, what we're
going to do today

01:48:40.860 --> 01:48:43.200 align:middle line:84%
is we're going to show
the entire GKR protocol,

01:48:43.200 --> 01:48:45.820 align:middle line:90%
assuming you can compute this.

01:48:45.820 --> 01:48:47.410 align:middle line:84%
And then only next
time, we're going

01:48:47.410 --> 01:48:49.120 align:middle line:84%
to show how to
compute this thing.

01:48:49.120 --> 01:48:53.570 align:middle line:84%
And actually, there's-- we're
going to show that next time.

01:48:53.570 --> 01:48:55.600 align:middle line:84%
How can we help them
compute it or how can we

01:48:55.600 --> 01:48:59.408 align:middle line:84%
compute in a different-- how
can we deal with this issue?

01:48:59.408 --> 01:49:01.200 align:middle line:84%
But for now, let's say
he can compute this.

01:49:01.200 --> 01:49:04.720 align:middle line:90%


01:49:04.720 --> 01:49:06.350 align:middle line:84%
So the verifier is
like, OK, let's see.

01:49:06.350 --> 01:49:07.680 align:middle line:90%
This I can definitely compute.

01:49:07.680 --> 01:49:08.970 align:middle line:90%
That's easy.

01:49:08.970 --> 01:49:10.950 align:middle line:84%
So the verifier
can compute this.

01:49:10.950 --> 01:49:13.570 align:middle line:90%
He can compute this.

01:49:13.570 --> 01:49:16.120 align:middle line:90%
He can compute this.

01:49:16.120 --> 01:49:17.530 align:middle line:90%
And what does he's left?

01:49:17.530 --> 01:49:21.400 align:middle line:84%
He's left with two
things he can't compute,

01:49:21.400 --> 01:49:23.830 align:middle line:90%
V tilde and i plus 1--

01:49:23.830 --> 01:49:27.810 align:middle line:84%
V tilde and i plus 1,
and point Z1 and Z2.

01:49:27.810 --> 01:49:30.880 align:middle line:90%
So now, how do we continue?

01:49:30.880 --> 01:49:32.680 align:middle line:84%
What we're going to
say is the following.

01:49:32.680 --> 01:49:38.200 align:middle line:84%
Suppose in this sum he was
cheated, namely the Vi value,

01:49:38.200 --> 01:49:41.470 align:middle line:90%
Vi star is not correct.

01:49:41.470 --> 01:49:43.660 align:middle line:84%
By the Sumcheck
protocol, it means

01:49:43.660 --> 01:49:46.060 align:middle line:84%
that this entire
value is also not

01:49:46.060 --> 01:49:48.398 align:middle line:84%
correct with very
high probability.

01:49:48.398 --> 01:49:49.940 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the probability
depends on f.

01:49:49.940 --> 01:49:51.890 align:middle line:84%
You'll take f large
enough that it can

01:49:51.890 --> 01:49:53.590 align:middle line:90%
be with very high probability.

01:49:53.590 --> 01:49:56.967 align:middle line:84%
So you're left
with a false claim.

01:49:56.967 --> 01:49:58.550 align:middle line:84%
Now, what the verifier
is going to do,

01:49:58.550 --> 01:50:00.883 align:middle line:84%
the verifier is going to tell
the prover, you know what?

01:50:00.883 --> 01:50:05.410 align:middle line:90%
Tell me what these two--

01:50:05.410 --> 01:50:07.660 align:middle line:84%
you gave me something I don't
know what to do with it.

01:50:07.660 --> 01:50:08.950 align:middle line:90%
Tell me the values.

01:50:08.950 --> 01:50:15.050 align:middle line:84%
You tell me the values of Vi
plus 1 tilde and Z1 and Z2.

01:50:15.050 --> 01:50:18.290 align:middle line:84%
So the verifier, the
prover comes in, OK, sure.

01:50:18.290 --> 01:50:28.346 align:middle line:84%
V tilde and i plus 1 and
Z1 is Vi plus 1 star 1,

01:50:28.346 --> 01:50:33.050 align:middle line:90%
and V tilde and Z2--

01:50:33.050 --> 01:50:38.030 align:middle line:84%
sorry, i plus 1, is
V star i plus 1, 2.

01:50:38.030 --> 01:50:40.740 align:middle line:90%
Here are the values.

01:50:40.740 --> 01:50:42.772 align:middle line:90%
Now I'm the verifier.

01:50:42.772 --> 01:50:43.480 align:middle line:90%
I'm the verifier.

01:50:43.480 --> 01:50:44.290 align:middle line:90%
What do I do?

01:50:44.290 --> 01:50:44.860 align:middle line:90%
I check.

01:50:44.860 --> 01:50:49.810 align:middle line:84%
I check that add times the
sum of these two values

01:50:49.810 --> 01:50:52.800 align:middle line:84%
you gave me plus mult times
the multiplication of these two

01:50:52.800 --> 01:50:56.270 align:middle line:84%
values you gave me
is equal what we got.

01:50:56.270 --> 01:51:00.150 align:middle line:84%
Now, by the soundness of
the Sumcheck protocol,

01:51:00.150 --> 01:51:02.070 align:middle line:90%
we know what we got is false.

01:51:02.070 --> 01:51:04.380 align:middle line:90%
It's not true.

01:51:04.380 --> 01:51:07.500 align:middle line:90%
So the only way--

01:51:07.500 --> 01:51:14.903 align:middle line:84%
if the prover gives the correct
thing, then he will fail.

01:51:14.903 --> 01:51:16.820 align:middle line:84%
If you give the correct
thing, I'll catch him.

01:51:16.820 --> 01:51:18.528 align:middle line:84%
Because if he gives
me the correct thing,

01:51:18.528 --> 01:51:21.240 align:middle line:90%
I get the true value.

01:51:21.240 --> 01:51:25.100 align:middle line:84%
But I'm going to check him
against the false value.

01:51:25.100 --> 01:51:26.820 align:middle line:90%
Again, what do we know?

01:51:26.820 --> 01:51:29.490 align:middle line:90%
He started with a false Vi star.

01:51:29.490 --> 01:51:33.220 align:middle line:84%
We did sum of such Sumcheck,
we're left with a false--

01:51:33.220 --> 01:51:35.430 align:middle line:84%
we said that this
is something false

01:51:35.430 --> 01:51:37.380 align:middle line:84%
because the sum of
the Sumcheck protocol

01:51:37.380 --> 01:51:39.520 align:middle line:84%
says if you started
with a sum that's false,

01:51:39.520 --> 01:51:41.880 align:middle line:84%
you'll get a false
claim about the f.

01:51:41.880 --> 01:51:44.740 align:middle line:84%
So now I get that this
thing is equal to some beta,

01:51:44.740 --> 01:51:47.700 align:middle line:90%
but this beta is not true.

01:51:47.700 --> 01:51:50.920 align:middle line:84%
So now I tell him, OK, I don't
know how to compute this thing.

01:51:50.920 --> 01:51:56.160 align:middle line:84%
Tell me what the two values in
the low-degree extensions are.

01:51:56.160 --> 01:51:58.320 align:middle line:84%
If you tell me this,
assuming I compute

01:51:58.320 --> 01:52:00.760 align:middle line:84%
this add tilde and
mult tilde for now,

01:52:00.760 --> 01:52:02.780 align:middle line:90%
then I compute this thing.

01:52:02.780 --> 01:52:04.700 align:middle line:84%
Now, the important
thing to note,

01:52:04.700 --> 01:52:10.150 align:middle line:84%
that if this value
was false, he must--

01:52:10.150 --> 01:52:11.680 align:middle line:84%
if he gives me the
true values, I'm

01:52:11.680 --> 01:52:14.260 align:middle line:84%
going to reject them because
I'm going to compute this thing

01:52:14.260 --> 01:52:18.130 align:middle line:84%
and check if it's what he
claimed it is after the Sumcheck

01:52:18.130 --> 01:52:19.640 align:middle line:90%
protocol.

01:52:19.640 --> 01:52:21.840 align:middle line:84%
And because he claimed
the false value,

01:52:21.840 --> 01:52:24.442 align:middle line:84%
if he give me the true things,
I'm going to reject him.

01:52:24.442 --> 01:52:26.150 align:middle line:84%
So the only thing he
can do if he doesn't

01:52:26.150 --> 01:52:28.400 align:middle line:84%
want to be rejected
off the bat is

01:52:28.400 --> 01:52:32.637 align:middle line:84%
to give me one of them
false, or both false.

01:52:32.637 --> 01:52:34.220 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So he has
to give two values.

01:52:34.220 --> 01:52:36.660 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Right.

01:52:36.660 --> 01:52:39.320 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

01:52:39.320 --> 01:52:40.010 align:middle line:90%
So where are we?

01:52:40.010 --> 01:52:41.800 align:middle line:90%
What did we do?

01:52:41.800 --> 01:52:47.010 align:middle line:84%
After a lot of sweat, we
started with a claim on layer i.

01:52:47.010 --> 01:52:49.900 align:middle line:84%
We did manage to get
down to layer i plus 1,

01:52:49.900 --> 01:52:51.260 align:middle line:90%
as I promised you guys.

01:52:51.260 --> 01:52:52.610 align:middle line:90%
Here's the price.

01:52:52.610 --> 01:52:54.690 align:middle line:84%
First, miraculously,
we assume he

01:52:54.690 --> 01:52:59.690 align:middle line:84%
can compute add tilde and mult
tilde on its own, Assumed Why?

01:52:59.690 --> 01:53:00.410 align:middle line:90%
Whatever.

01:53:00.410 --> 01:53:05.720 align:middle line:84%
Second, we went from
one claim about layer i

01:53:05.720 --> 01:53:08.460 align:middle line:84%
to two claims about
layer i plus 1.

01:53:08.460 --> 01:53:10.828 align:middle line:84%
That doesn't sound
very reassuring.

01:53:10.828 --> 01:53:12.370 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Isn't that
where we started?

01:53:12.370 --> 01:53:14.070 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Yeah.

01:53:14.070 --> 01:53:16.290 align:middle line:90%
YAEL KALAI: Exactly.

01:53:16.290 --> 01:53:17.285 align:middle line:90%
It's where we started.

01:53:17.285 --> 01:53:20.500 align:middle line:90%


01:53:20.500 --> 01:53:23.220 align:middle line:84%
This is a great
time for a break.